    {topic:"",q:"When a conductor is cut by magnetic lines of force an EMF is induced. This is.",opts:["Kirkchoff's Law.","Faraday's Law.","Lenz's Law."],ans:1,exp:"Faraday's Law is induced EMF is directly proportional to the rate of change of flux."},
    {topic:"",q:"The direction of induced conventional current in a wire rotating in a magnetic field can be determined by.",opts:["cork screw rule.","Fleming's right hand rule.","Fleming's left hand rule."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"How would you find the direction of the electron flow in an armature located in a magnetic field?.",opts:["Flemings left hand rule.","Flemings right hand rule.","Maxwells corkscrew rule."],ans:0,exp:"Assuming conventional current flow."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the SI unit of conductance?.",opts:["Ohm.","Siemen.","Ohm-metre."],ans:1,exp:"Conductance is 1/R and is measured in Siemens."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the SI unit of resistivity?.",opts:["Ohms.","Ohm metre.","Ohms/metre."],ans:1,exp:"Resistivity is ohm metre (transpose the resistance formula R = ρL / A, and see what units it gives)."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the formula for conductance? (R=Resistance).",opts:["R + 1.","1/R.","1-R."],ans:1,exp:"Conductance is the inverse of resistance."},
    {topic:"",q:"In electrical circuit, Direct Current power is represented by the.",opts:["Ampere.","Watt.","Joule."],ans:1,exp:"DC power is measured in Watts."},
    {topic:"",q:"In S.I. units, work is denoted by.",opts:["Newton-meter which is Joules.","Joules/sec which is Watts.","Kilogram-force-meter/sec which is Metric Horsepower."],ans:0,exp:"Work has the same unit as energy, i.e. Nm or Joule."},
    {topic:"",q:"The unit of energy is the.",opts:["Joule.","Watt.","Coulomb."],ans:0,exp:"Energy is measured in Joules."},
    {topic:"",q:"Potential difference is another term for.",opts:["voltage.","charge.","energy."],ans:0,exp:"Potential difference, electromotive force, electrical pressure, are all terms for voltage."},
    {topic:"",q:"XL is the symbol of.",opts:["inductive reactance.","capacitive reactance.","impedance."],ans:0,exp:"'L' is for inductance, 'X' is for reactance."},
    {topic:"",q:"Using Ohm's law.",opts:["current is directly proportional to the resistance.","current is directly proportional to the EMF.","resistance is directly proportional to EMF."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"One volt is induced into a circuit with a current varying at 1 amp per second. The circuit has.",opts:["1 ampere turn.","1 Tesla.","1 Henry."],ans:2,exp:"1 Henry = 1 Volt per Amp per Second."},
    {topic:"",q:"The property of a conductor of electricity that limits or restricts the flow of electric current is.",opts:["limiter.","resistance.","fuse."],ans:1,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics by Eismin, Chapter 6, pg 101."},
    {topic:"",q:"Kirchhoff’s law is applicable to.",opts:["parallel networks.","series networks.","closed loop networks."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The SI unit of work is the.",opts:["Watt.","Newton Meter.","Joule."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The EMF of a generator can be calculated from which law?.",opts:["Faraday's law.","Kirchhoff’s law.","Lenz's law."],ans:0,exp:"04. Generation of Electricity."},
    {topic:"",q:"Thermocouple harnesses are made from the same materials as the thermocouple so that.",opts:["mini junctions are not formed.","the resistance is not increased beyond limits.","they will not corrode."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Point A on the armature of the generator shown is producing.",opts:["minimum current.","zero current.","maximum current."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A piezoelectric device generates electricity through.",opts:["friction.","light.","pressure."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A photozoidal cell produces electricity when subjected to.",opts:["light.","pressure.","heat."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A thermocouple indicator is basically a type of.",opts:["millivoltmeter.","milliammeter.","milliohmeter."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A light/heat sensitive cell is a.",opts:["transistor.","diode.","transducer."],ans:2,exp:"Any transducer turns one energy type into another energy type. In this case light or heat into electricity."},
    {topic:"",q:"A component that produces an output voltage due to incident light is called a.",opts:["liquid crystal.","solar cell.","light emitting diode."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The flux density of a magnetic field is 1.5T. The length of the conductor in the field is 2 m and the speed of the conductor is 10 m/s. The EMF induced is.",opts:["0.3 V.","7.5 V.","30 V."],ans:2,exp:"EMF = Blv = 1.5 * 2 * 10 = 30V."},
    {topic:"",q:"A conductor is placed in a magnetised coil. What will happen?.",opts:["Nothing will happen unless the conductor is moved.","The conductor will move.","A force will be created."],ans:0,exp:"Faraday's Law."},
    {topic:"",q:"The maximum output voltage from a basic single loop generator is when the loop is.",opts:["rotating anti-clockwise relative to the direction of the magnetic flux.","cutting maximum lines of magnetic flux.","at 45 o to the magnetic flux."],ans:1,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics Eismin Page 83. 05. DC Sources of Electricity."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 20 cell battery with each cell having an internal resistance of 0.1 ohms is charged with 2 leads having a total resistance of 0.1 ohms. The battery is charged with a current of 5 amps. What is the charging voltage?.",opts:["0.5 volts.","10.5 volts.","0.005 volts."],ans:1,exp:"Total battery internal resistance = 0.1 * 20 = 2 ohms, Total circuit resistance, including leads = 2 + 0.1 = 2.1 ohms, V = I * R = 5A * 2.1 ohms = 10.5V."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two 10V, 20 Ah batteries are connected in parallel and connected across a 10 ohm load. How long could they supply normal current before the voltage begins to decay?.",opts:["40 hours.","20 hours.","4 hours."],ans:0,exp:"Use Ohms law to work out the current (1A). Two batteries in parallel then rating is doubled (40 Ah)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A battery rated at 40 Ah will supply 200 mA for.",opts:["200 hours.","20 hours.","5 hours."],ans:0,exp:"200 mA = 0.2 A. 40 Ah / 0.2 A = 200 hours."},
    {topic:"",q:"A zinc-carbon battery life depends upon.",opts:["the amount of zinc.","the purity of the carbon rod.","the amount of the electrolyte paste."],ans:0,exp:"The zinc is slowly eaten away in a zinc carbon battery (primary cell)."},
    {topic:"",q:"When checking the SG of the electrolyte in a lead acid battery, you should.",opts:["check any cell because they will all be the same.","check only the no. 1 cell because it is the master cell.","check all cells because they may be different."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The PD at the terminals of an open circuit battery with a small internal resistance will be.",opts:["more than the EMF.","less than the EMF.","the same as the EMF."],ans:2,exp:"EMF is the battery voltage (i.e. that which is written on the side of it) and the PD is the actual terminal voltage. If it is open circuit, no current flows to drop a voltage across the internal resistance so they are the same."},
    {topic:"",q:"An accumulation of hydrogen on the plates of a battery is known as.",opts:["polarization.","ionization.","hydration."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What part of a battery is covered in hydrogen during polarization?.",opts:["Anode.","Both the anode and the cathode.","Cathode."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Which of the following is most likely to cause thermal runaway in a nickel-cadmium battery?.",opts:["A high internal resistance condition.","High current charging of the battery to more than 100 percent of its capacity.","Excessive current draw from the battery."],ans:1,exp:"Thermal runaway usually occurs on charging."},
    {topic:"",q:"The method of ascertaining the voltage of a standard aircraft lead-acid battery is by checking.",opts:["the voltage with rated load switched ON.","the voltage on open circuit.","the voltage off load."],ans:2,exp:"If just checking the voltage (as opposed to a charge test) then the battery must be off load to prevent the internal resistance dropping the terminal voltage."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two batteries 12V and 40 Ah each, are in series. What is the total capacity?.",opts:["12V 80 Ah.","24V 40 Ah.","24V 80Ah."],ans:1,exp:"Batteries in series - voltage increases but capacity remains the same (vice versa if they were in parallel)."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a battery has got low internal resistance, then the.",opts:["no load voltage will be the same as on load voltage.","on load voltage will be greater than no load voltage.","no load voltage will be greater than on load voltage."],ans:2,exp:"The internal resistance will always drop the voltage and reduce the terminal voltage. It is the same whether it is high or low internal resistance - just a matter of 'how much'."},
    {topic:"",q:"Conventional current flow inside a battery is from.",opts:["either anode to cathode or cathode to anode, depending on the active elements.","cathode to anode.","anode to cathode."],ans:2,exp:"The cathode is positive. Conventional current flows from positive to negative on the external circuit, but to close the circuit must flow negative to positive INSIDE the battery."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a voltaic cell, what is the build-up on the cathode called?.",opts:["Hydration.","Polarization.","Sulphation."],ans:2,exp:"Polarization is build-up of hydrogen on the ANODE. Sulphation however, is build-up of lead-sulphate on BOTH anode and cathode."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two 12V 40 amp hour batteries connected in parallel will produce.",opts:["24V 80 ah.","12V 80 ah.","24V 40 ah."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A 24-volt source is required to furnish 48 watts to a parallel circuit consisting of four resistors of equal value. What is the voltage drop across each resistor?.",opts:["12 volts.","24 volts.","3 volts."],ans:1,exp:"Resistors ion parallel, voltage across each one is the same and equal to the source voltage (if nothing else is in series with them)."},
    {topic:"",q:"The method of ascertaining the voltage of a standard aircraft lead-acid battery is by checking.",opts:["the voltage on open circuit.","the voltage off load.","the voltage with rated load switched ON."],ans:1,exp:"Assuming it is just a voltage check (and not a charge test) then it must be off load when carrying out the test. Otherwise the internal resistance will drop the voltage by an amount which varies according to the external load."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two similar 12v batteries connected in parallel will produce.",opts:["24v e.m.f. with the same capacity as each battery.","12V e.m.f. with twice the capacity of each battery.","24V e.m.f. with twice the capacity of each battery"],ans:1,exp:"Batteries in parallel - voltage remains the same but the current rating is increased."},
    {topic:"",q:"The voltage of a secondary cell is.",opts:["determined by the active materials on the plates.","determined by the number of plates.","determined by the area of the plates."],ans:0,exp:"Voltage of a battery is determined by what the plates are made from, and how many plates are in series. However, a 'cell' is not a battery and can only ever have two plates."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the ampere-hour rating of a storage battery that is designed to deliver 45 amperes for 2.5 hours?.",opts:["112.5 ampere-hour.","90.0 ampere-hour.","45.0 ampere-hour."],ans:0,exp:"Ampere.hours means amps * hours."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a nicad battery is not to be used immediately it should be stored.",opts:["dry.","fully charged.","fully dissipated."],ans:2,exp:"Ni-cad batteries must be stored fully discharged. Often with a metal bar across their terminals to ensure they are fully dissipated."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a mercury cell the steel casing is.",opts:["the negative terminal.","neither.","the positive terminal."],ans:2,exp:"The 'outer can' is in contact with the zinc anode, which is positive."},
    {topic:"",q:"To improve the life of a dry cell it would be manufactured with.",opts:["more electrolyte paste.","more zinc.","a pure carbon positive rod."],ans:1,exp:"The zinc (negative electrode or cathode) gets eaten away."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a simple voltaic cell the collection of ions on the cathode causes.",opts:["polarisation.","sulphation.","hydration."],ans:1,exp:"The formation of lead sulphate on both plates of a lead acid battery is called SULPHATION."},
    {topic:"",q:"The electrolyte in a nicad battery would rise if the battery was.",opts:["remaining at constant voltage.","charging.","discharging."],ans:1,exp:"The physical level of electrolyte drops a as Ni-Cad battery discharges."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two 2 volt 10AH cells are connected in series, the output voltage and the capacity would be.",opts:["4 volt 10 AH.","4 volt 20 AH.","2 volt, 20 AH."],ans:0,exp:"Batteries in series - voltage doubles, but the rating stays the same."},
    {topic:"",q:"The electrolyte in a nicad battery is.",opts:["potassium hydroxide.","nickel hydroxide.","cadmium hydroxide."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a Leclanche cell the plates are made of.",opts:["mercury and lithium.","nickel and cadmium.","zinc and carbon."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a simple voltaic cell, polarization reduces output voltage because of the.",opts:["build up of hydrogen on the cathode.","transfer of material between the anode and the cathode.","build up of hydrogen on the anode."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When the temperature of the electrolyte in a battery increases, the SG.",opts:["remains the same.","goes up.","goes down."],ans:2,exp:"Volume rises with temperature, but mass does not. SG is a measure of the electrolyte density (which is mass / volume)."},
    {topic:"",q:"The Daniel Cell electrodes are.",opts:["copper and zinc.","carbon and zinc.","zinc and manganese dioxide."],ans:0,exp:"The Daniel Cell was an improvement on the first ever cell made by Voltaire. Both used copper and zinc electrodes."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a thermocouple, where is voltage measured?.",opts:["At both junctions.","At the hot junction.","At the cold junction."],ans:2,exp:"The voltmeter measures the voltmeter across the cold junction of a thermocouple."},
    {topic:"",q:"Mercury cells are covered with a metal cover.",opts:["as a protective cover.","as a negative terminal.","as a positive terminal."],ans:2,exp:"The outer cover (or case) of a mercury cell is the positive terminal - Have a look at your watch or calculator battery to verify."},
    {topic:"",q:"The capacity of a battery is measured in.",opts:["volts.","ampere-hours.","cubic centimetres."],ans:1,exp:"Battery capacity is in amperehours."},
    {topic:"",q:"A battery's internal resistance is determined by (OCV = Open Circuit Voltage, CCV = Closed Circuit Voltage).",opts:["IR = (OCV – CCV)/ i.","IR = (OCV + CCV) / i.","IR = (OCV - CCV) / V."],ans:0,exp:"Internal resistance (IR) has no effect on output voltage when Open Circuit. So Open Circuit Voltage (ocv) is higher than Closed Circuit Voltage (ccv). Using ohm's law (R=V/I), IR = (ocv) - ccv) all divided by current."},
    {topic:"",q:"There are more negative than positive plates in a lead acid battery because.",opts:["it is necessary for chemical action to take place.","it reduces the internal resistance of the cell.","the positive plates are prone to warping if the chemical action is taking place only on one side."],ans:2,exp:"A lead acid battery normally has one more negative plate than positive plate because the positive plates are prone to warping if the chemical action is taking place only on one side."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the approximate internal resistance of a Leclanche cell?.",opts:["0.02 ohms - 4 ohms.","6 ohms - 10 ohms.","20 ohms - 30 ohms."],ans:0,exp:"A leclanche cell is just another name for the zinc-carbon cell. Its IR is more like 0.4 - 4 ohms. Some good info on the types of cells and their IRs at"},
    {topic:"",q:"An aircraft battery on charge from the aircraft generators causes.",opts:["battery voltage to increases to nominal voltage and the charge current decreases.","internal resistance of the battery to decrease in sympathy with the decreasing charging current.","battery voltage to decrease to zero until it is fully charged."],ans:2,exp:"As the battery voltage increases, the current charging it decreases."},
    {topic:"",q:"What effect does hydrogen have in a battery cell?.",opts:["Sulphation.","Nothing.","Polarization."],ans:2,exp:"Hydrogen, if allowed to form on the anode, reduces the battery output. This is polarization."},
    {topic:"",q:"To generate electricity with a fuel cell, you need a constant supply of.",opts:["Nitrogen & Sunlight.","Oxygen & Hydrogen.","Kerosene & Heat."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A ni-cad battery found to be below normal capacity should.",opts:["have its electrolyte adjusted.","be removed and used for ground use only.","be re-charged with a freshening charge."],ans:2,exp:"See Jeppesen's Aircraft Batteries."},
    {topic:"",q:"In cold weather, to prevent the electrolyte freezing, a battery must be kept.",opts:["fully charged.","does not matter.","fully discharged."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The internal resistance of a battery off load compared to on load is.",opts:["the same.","increased.","decreased."],ans:0,exp:"Internal resistance is constant, regardless of load."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two 12V, 40Ah cells in series gives.",opts:["24V 80Ah.","12V 40Ah.","24V 40Ah."],ans:2,exp:"Cells in series, voltage doubles, Amp hour rating remains the same."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 24V 40AH battery discharges at 200mA. How long will it last?.",opts:["400 hours.","300 hours.","200 hours."],ans:2,exp:"200mA goes into 1A-5 times. 5 * 40 = 200 hours."},
    {topic:"",q:"The electrolyte level of a ni-cad battery.",opts:["falls during charge.","falls during discharge.","rises during discharge."],ans:1,exp:"The electrolyte level of a nicad battery falls during discharge (but the s.g. remains constant)."},
    {topic:"",q:"When light energises a component, what is the component?.",opts:["Light emitting diode.","Photodiode.","Laser diode."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Formation of white crystals of potassium carbonate on a properly serviced Ni-cd battery indicates.",opts:["over charged.","full charged.","under charged."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Thermal runaway causes.",opts:["violent gassing only.","violent gassing, boiling of electrolyte and melting of plates and casing.","no gassing, but boiling of electrolyte and melting of plates and casing."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A primary cell.",opts:["can not recharged.","can be recharged but only a few times.","can be recharged."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the insulation resistance of a lead/acid battery is down, it indicates.",opts:["case leakage.","leakage between positive and negative terminals.","intercell leakage."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When the battery is connected to the aircraft, which terminal should you connect first?.",opts:["Any.","Positive.","Negative."],ans:1,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics Eismin Page 43."},
    {topic:"",q:"An ammeter in a battery charging system is for the purpose of indicating the.",opts:["rate of current used to charge the battery.","amperage available for use.","total amperes being used in the aeroplane."],ans:0,exp:"Whilst charging (as the question says) the ammeter shows current flow TO the battery."},
    {topic:"",q:"For how many hours will a 140AH battery deliver 15A?.",opts:["9.33 hours.","27.25 hours.","15.15 hours."],ans:0,exp:"15 * H = 140, H = 9.33 hrs."},
    {topic:"",q:"What part of a nickel-cadmium cell helps to prevent thermal runaway?.",opts:["The separator.","The negative plate.","The positive plate."],ans:0,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics Eismin Page 47 2nd para."},
    {topic:"",q:"When charging current is applied to a nickel-cadmium battery, the cells emit gas only.",opts:["when the electrolyte level is low.","if the cells are defective.","toward the end of the charging cycle."],ans:2,exp:"EEL/1-3 4.5.5."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the internal resistance of a battery?.",opts:["The resistance measured across the two terminals.","The resistance measured when the battery is half charged.","The resistance present inside the battery while connected to a load."],ans:2,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics page 33."},
    {topic:"",q:"A potential difference of 50 volts produces a current of 10 milliamperes through a resistance of.",opts:["500 ohms.","5 ohms.","5,000 ohms."],ans:2,exp:"PD = 50V, I = 0.01A, R =?, R=PD/I."},
    {topic:"",q:"A conductor with a positive Q (charge) of 4C has 12.56 x 1018 electrons added to it. It will have a Q =.",opts:["6.","2.","zero."],ans:0,exp:"A charge of 1 Coulomb (C) is 6.28 * 1018. Hence 4 + 2 = 6 C."},
    {topic:"",q:"The power dissipated by a load of known resistance connected across a known potential difference can be calculated by.",opts:["dividing the square of the potential difference by the value of the load resistance.","multiplying the square of the potential difference by the value of the load resistance.","working out the current flowing in the circuit by using ohms law and multiplying the result by the resistance."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A current of 5A flows for 2 minutes. How many coulombs passed a point in the circuit?.",opts:["2.5.","600.","100."],ans:1,exp:"Q = IT, Q = 5 * 120 = 600C."},
    {topic:"",q:"A circuit has a current flow of 6A. If the voltage is trebled, the new current will be.",opts:["6A.","18A.","2A."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A 10V battery supplies a resistive load of 10 ohms for 1 minute. What is the work done?.",opts:["60J.","600J.","10J."],ans:1,exp:"Work out power. Then Energy = Power * Time (time must be in seconds)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A galvanometer measures.",opts:["millivolts.","megohms.","milliamps."],ans:2,exp:"A galvanometer measures milliamps."},
    {topic:"",q:"The voltage at point A is.",opts:["28 V.","21 V.","7 V."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A loss of electrical insulation results in.",opts:["an open circuit between the supply and earth.","a short circuit between the supply and earth.","an open circuit in the supply."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A 3, 5 and 2 ohms resistance is connected in series with a 10 V battery. The voltage across the 2 ohms resistor is.",opts:["2 V.","10V.","4 V."],ans:0,exp:"Each resistor takes its relative share of the voltage drop. Total resistance is 10 ohms, so the 2 ohm resistor takes 2/10ths of the voltage."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the PD of a circuit which has a 40 mA current and a 1 kilohm resistance?.",opts:["40 V.","400 V.","4 V."],ans:0,exp:"V = I x R. mA * kohms cancel each others' prefix."},
    {topic:"",q:"Three branches in a circuit have currents entering of 3A, 4A and 5A. A forth branch has 10 A leaving. A fifth branch must have.",opts:["22A leaving.","2A leaving.","2A entering."],ans:1,exp:"Basic Kirkchoff's current law. Sum of currents entering = sum of currents leaving."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a circuit containing three resistors of equal value connected in parallel, one resistor goes open circuit. The current in the other two resistors will.",opts:["decrease.","increase.","remain the same."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"20 amperes flow for 20 seconds. How many coulombs have flowed?.",opts:["1.","20.","400."],ans:2,exp:"Q = It."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the resistance of an electrical circuit is increased.",opts:["the current will increase.","the voltage will increase.","the current will decrease."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If 2 coulombs flowed through a circuit in 2 seconds, the circuit would have.",opts:["1 amp.","2 volts PD.","4 amps."],ans:0,exp:"Q = It so I = Q/t."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the voltage across a resistor is doubled.",opts:["the current is doubled.","the current is halved.","the resistance is halved."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The total current flowing in a circuit of 200 lamps in parallel, each of a resistance of 400 ohm and connected across an input of 100 volts is.",opts:["25 amps.","50 amps.","40 amps."],ans:1,exp:"Total resistance is 400 / 200 = 2 ohms. Ohms law for current I = V/R = 100/2 = 50 amps."},
    {topic:"",q:"If service No. 1 is isolated from the supply busbar shown there will be.",opts:["an increase in supply voltage.","a decrease in total current consumption.","a decrease in supply voltage."],ans:1,exp:"Shutting down a service on an aircraft (by pulling the circuit breaker for example), increases the total resistance and reduces the current consumption."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the cross- sectional area of a conductor is doubled, with voltage constant, the current will.",opts:["remain constant.","double.","halve."],ans:1,exp:"Get the formula for resistance of a conductor - and apply ohms law to it."},
    {topic:"",q:"If two resistors of 5 and 10 ohm respectively are connected in series and the current in the 5 ohm resistor is 1A. what is the current in the 10 ohm resistor?.",opts:["1 amp.","It cannot be found without knowing the applied voltage.","1/3 amp."],ans:0,exp:"Resistors in series - current is the same in each one."},
    {topic:"",q:"The voltage in a series circuit.",opts:["is different in each component.","is the same in each component.","is less than it would be in a parallel circuit."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If voltage is 100V, resistance is 25 ohms, what is the current?.",opts:["4 amperes.","0.4 amperes.","2500 amperes."],ans:0,exp:"Ohms Law I = V/R."},
    {topic:"",q:"A short circuit between the supply and earth.",opts:["is not dangerous as the current drawn will be low.","does not matter if the circuit uses the aircraft earth as a return.","could be very dangerous as the current drawn will be very high."],ans:2,exp:"In a short circuit, the resistance is zero. By ohms law, I = V/R. If R = 0, I = V/0 so what is I (it is not zero!!!)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A circuit consists of 3 ohm, 5 ohm and 12 ohm resistors in series. The current flowing in the 5 ohm resistor is 10 amps. What is the applied voltage?.",opts:["10 V.","100 V.","200 V."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Two resistors are connected in series and have an e.m.f. of V volts across them. If the voltages across the resistances are V1 and V2 then by Kirchhoff's law.",opts:["V2 = V1 + V.","V1 = V2 + V.","V = V1 + V2."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A voltmeter is connected.",opts:["in parallel.","in series or parallel.","in series."],ans:0,exp:"Voltmeters are connected 'across' the component (i.e. in parallel with it. Ammeters are connected in series."},
    {topic:"",q:"Since electrical supplies taken from a bus-bar are in parallel, isolating some of the services would.",opts:["reduce the current consumption from the bus-bar.","increase the current consumption from the bus-bar.","not affect the current consumption, it would reduce the voltage."],ans:0,exp:"Isolating some of the services increases the resistance of the total circuit similar to removing a resistor from a parallel circuit. So total current consumption reduces."},
    {topic:"",q:"The current flowing through a circuit can be increased to four times its original value by.",opts:["doubling the applied voltage and halving the resistance.","doubling the resistance and doubling the applied voltage.","halving the applied voltage and halving the resistance."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a circuit containing three resistors of equal value connected in series and one of the resistors short circuits, the effect is for the current in the other two resistors to.",opts:["decrease.","increase.","remain the same."],ans:1,exp:"Reducing one resistor to zero ohms (short circuit) means the source emf is now applied to the two remaining resistors, and causes a greater current flow through them."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a series resistive circuit.",opts:["the total voltage is equal to the sum of the individual voltages.","the total voltage is the same as the highest individual.","the total voltage equals the difference between the individual voltages."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An ammeter is connected into a circuit in.",opts:["series.","shunt.","parallel."],ans:0,exp:"ammeter is always put into a circuit in series with the component it is measuring the current through."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the voltage at A?.",opts:["26V.","2V.","28V."],ans:0,exp:"4 ohm resistor will drop 4/62ths of the 28V supply (i.e. 1.8V). So there will be approximately 26V left."},
    {topic:"",q:"The unknown current in the network below is.",opts:["22A.","3A.","47A."],ans:1,exp:"Kirchhoff's current law."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a balanced Wheatstone bridge, across the centre of the bridge there is.",opts:["current and voltage at maximum.","no current flow.","no voltage present at either end."],ans:1,exp:"The ammeter reads zero when it is balanced."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 24V battery has an internal resistance of 1 ohm. When connected to a load, 12 amps flows. The value of the load is.",opts:["12 ohms.","1/2 ohm.","1 ohm."],ans:2,exp:"Total R = V/I = 24/12 = 2 ohms. Subtract the internal resistance to get the external resistance (i.e. the load)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A parallel circuit with any number of 2 terminal connections.",opts:["the individual voltage drops is equal to the emf.","has the same current throughout.","the resistance is dependent on current."],ans:0,exp:"Parallel circuit - the voltage drops across each branch is the source voltage (regardless of the resistance in the branches)."},
    {topic:"",q:"To find the internal resistance of a battery in a circuit of known current.",opts:["use the formula R = V * I.","find the lost volts of the circuit.","find the emf of the circuit."],ans:1,exp:"Find the difference between the Voltage On-load and the Voltage Off-load (the volts 'lost' across the internal resistance), then use R = V/I (Ohm's Law)."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a series circuit with 3 resistors, the current is.",opts:["lower than the smallest.","the total of all 3 using ohms law to find the current.","even in all 3."],ans:2,exp:"The current is the same in all components in a series circuit."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the voltage dropped across resistor 'A'?.",opts:["2 V.","20 V.","10 V."],ans:1,exp:"28-(2 + 6) = 20 V (voltage across both the parallel resistors is the same 20 V)."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the current through resistor C?.",opts:["4.5A.","18A.","9A."],ans:0,exp:"You need to know what the ohmic value of resistor C is to know the current through it, or at least the relative value of C compared to D - as the 9A current splits through C and D. It is 9A/2 (4.5A) only if C and D are the same. Besides, it cannot ever be 9A or 18A."},
    {topic:"",q:"Which is not thermally operated?.",opts:["A limiting resistor.","A current limiter.","A fuse."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a Desynn indicator system, the rotor is.",opts:["an electromagnet.","a permanent magnet.","an AC magnet."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A load that is subject to a potential difference with a current running through it will.",opts:["have a magnetic field around it with the magnitude and direction of the field determined by the corkscrew rule.","dissipate power in the form of heat.","will move in a direction shown by the right hand rule."],ans:1,exp:"07a. Resistance/Resistor."},
    {topic:"",q:"The resistance of a material is.",opts:["independent of the material type.","the same as its conductance.","the reciprocal of its conductance."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Resistance is measured using what unit of temperature?.",opts:["Absolute.","Centigrade.","Fahrenheit."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A resistor has 4 bands on it coloured blue, yellow, yellow, gold. It’s value is.",opts:["640 kΩ ± 5%.","6.4 mΩ ± 10%.","64 kΩ ± 5%."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The electron flow through a conductor will be decreased the most if the cross sectional area.",opts:["is decreased and the length is increased.","and the length are both decreased.","and the length are both increased."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Small resistors too small or mis-shapen for the application of colour are marked instead using.",opts:["an abbreviated resistance value.","a letter code.","a dot code."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Total resistance in a parallel resistor circuit, of R1 and R2 is.",opts:["RT = 1/R1 + 1/R2.","1/RT = (R1 * R2) ÷ (R1 + R2).","1/RT = 1/R1 + 1/R2."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the resistance of a resistor which is in series with two other resistors is doubled.",opts:["the current in that resistance is doubled.","the volts drop across that resistor increases.","the current in that resistance is halved."],ans:1,exp:"The current will reduce, but not halved, since it depends on how big the other two resistors are in relation to it. It will however take a greater share of the voltage drop in the circuit."},
    {topic:"",q:"Potentiometers are used as a.",opts:["variable voltage source.","variable resistor.","variable current source."],ans:1,exp:"Potentiometers are not a 'source' of voltage."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a Wheatstone Bridge, the ammeter reads zero, and the variable resistor is adjusted to 5 ohms. Resistor P has a value of 10 ohms and Q has a value of 100 ohms. What is the value of the unknown resistor?.",opts:["5 ohms.","0.5 ohms.","50 ohms."],ans:1,exp:"R = Rv * P/Q."},
    {topic:"",q:"In general, increasing the cross sectional area of an electrical cable.",opts:["increases its resistance.","enables it to carry more voltage.","enables it to carry more current."],ans:2,exp:"Get the formula for resistance of a cable. If you increase the cross sectional area, its resistance will decrease and it can carry more current. 'Carrying voltage' is not technically correct terminology, and besides, Any size cable can 'carry' any size voltage, but it is the current which will destroy it."},
    {topic:"",q:"Six resistors each of 6 ohms would be.",opts:["1 ohm in series.","1 ohm in parallel.","36 ohms in parallel."],ans:1,exp:"Get the formulas for resistors in parallel and resistors in series. Or, remember that resistors in series, total is greater than the largest,"},
    {topic:"",q:"A potentiometer varies.",opts:["resistance.","current.","voltage."],ans:2,exp:"A potentiometer is basically a variable voltage splitter."},
    {topic:"",q:"The formula for resistance in series is.",opts:["RT = R1 + R2 + R3.....Rn.","RT = R1 * R2 * R3.....Rn.","1/RT = 1/( R1 * R2 * R3.....Rn)."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the combined value of resistances of 4 ohm and 6 ohm connected in parallel?.",opts:["0.24 ohm.","2.4 ohm.","24 ohm."],ans:1,exp:"Resistors in parallel, the total resistance will be less than the smallest (but only slightly less)."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a number of resistors are connected in parallel, the total resistance is.",opts:["smaller than the lowest.","the same as the lowest.","greater than the lowest."],ans:0,exp:"Resistors in parallel. Total resistance is smaller than the lowest single resistor."},
    {topic:"",q:"The resistance to electrical flow in a wire depends on.",opts:["the diameter, length, material of wire and temperature.","the material only - copper or aluminium.","the length and material of the wire only."],ans:0,exp:"Get the formula for resistance of a conductor, and don't forget that most materials have a positive temperature coefficient."},
    {topic:"",q:"When resistors are in parallel the total current is equal to.",opts:["the current through one resistor.","the sum of the currents.","the reciprocal of all the currents."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Which of these will cause the resistance of a conductor to decrease?.",opts:["Decrease the length or the cross- sectional area.","Increase the length or decrease the cross-sectional area.","Decrease the length or increase the cross-sectional area."],ans:2,exp:"Get the formula for resistance of a conductor."},
    {topic:"",q:"If 2 resistors, one red, yellow, black, gold and the other violet, blue, black, silver were replaced by a single resistor. Ignoring tolerance, what would the colour coding be?.",opts:["Brown, black, brown.","Black, brown, black.","Brown, black, black."],ans:0,exp:"The red/yellow/black resistor is 24 ohms (get a colour code chart). The violet/blue/black resistor is 76 ohms. Total is 100 ohms. A 100 ohm resistor is brown/black/brown."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 47 kilohm resistor has the following colour code:.",opts:["Yellow, Violet, Orange.","Red, Orange, Yellow.","Orange, Violet, Red."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A 300 ohm resistor would have a colour code of.",opts:["orange, brown, black.","orange, orange, brown.","orange, black brown."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In this circuit, Rx is equal to.",opts:["R2 * R4 ÷ R3.","R3 * R4 ÷ R2.","R3 * R2 ÷ R4."],ans:2,exp:"Assuming the diagram is incomplete (no cross branch with a zeroed ammeter as per a Wheatstone bridge) Take Rx/R3 = R2/R4 and transpose."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a parallel circuit containing resistors.",opts:["the sum of the voltage drops equals applied voltage.","the voltage is the same in all parts of the circuit.","resistance is determined by value of current flow."],ans:1,exp:"In a parallel circuit, the voltage across each resistor is the same."},
    {topic:"",q:"Five different value resistors all have the same voltage dropped across them. How are the resistors connected?.",opts:["In Series/Parallel.","In Series.","In Parallel."],ans:2,exp:"Resistors in parallel with each other all have the same voltage drop."},
    {topic:"",q:"Conductance of a circuit is 2 milliMhos. What is its resistance?.",opts:["5000 Ohms.","500 Ohms.","5 Megohms."],ans:1,exp:"Conductance is the ease at which current flows. It is the inverse of resistance. (The unit 'Mho' is 'Ohm' backwards). Conductance = 2/1000 Mhos. Therefore Resistance = 1000/2 Ohms."},
    {topic:"",q:"When light hits a photodiode, its resistance.",opts:["stays the same.","increases.","decreases."],ans:2,exp:"Resistance decreases with light intensity."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 2 Megohm resistor can be written.",opts:["2M0F.","20MF.","M20F."],ans:0,exp:"Sometimes the multiplier, like M (for meg), or K (for kilo) or R (for 1) is put in place of the decimal point. The last letter is the tolerance. See Forum for more info."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a Wheatstone bridge at balance the galvanometer reads zero.",opts:["amps.","ohms.","volts."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The unit for resistivity is the.",opts:["ohms/square meter.","ohms/meter.","ohm.meter."],ans:2,exp:"stance"},
    {topic:"",q:"A 47 Kilohm resistor with a 10% tolerance has the following colour code:.",opts:["Yellow, Violet, Orange, Silver.","Orange, Violet, Red, Gold.","Red, Orange, Yellow, Silver."],ans:0,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics Eismin Page 100."},
    {topic:"",q:"The total resistance in a circuit is greater than the least resistor. This is true for a.",opts:["series and parallel circuit.","series circuit only.","parallel circuit only.","4 ohms."],ans:3,exp:"R = 2 * 8/4. Read up on Wheatstone bridges."},
    {topic:"",q:"A potentiometer has which of the following properties?.",opts:["Wire wound.","3 terminals.","Preset values."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Copper is an inferior conductor to aluminium when comparing.",opts:["CSA with CSA.","weight for weight.","load for load."],ans:1,exp:"Copper is a better inductor but aluminium is much lighter. CSA = cross sectional area (not Child Support Agency)."},
    {topic:"",q:"What happens to the resistance of a copper conductor when the temperature increases?.",opts:["It decreases.","It increases.","It remains the same."],ans:1,exp:"All materials excepts semiconductors have a positive temperature coefficient."},
    {topic:"",q:"Carbon has a.",opts:["temperature coefficient of zero.","positive temperature coefficient.","negative temperature coefficient."],ans:2,exp:"Carbon is a semiconductor, so its resistance reduces with temperature."},
    {topic:"",q:"The 5th coloured band on a resistor represents the.",opts:["reliability or temperature coefficient.","tolerance.","multiplier."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the temperature of a pure metal is reduced to absolute zero, its resistance will be.",opts:["unaffected.","practically zero.","infinity."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is represented by this diagram?.",opts:["Thermistor.","Rheostat.","Potentiometer."],ans:2,exp:"A potentiometer has 3 connections, a rheostat has only 2."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a resistor is too small or misshapen to fit the colour code bands, how else is it's value indicated?.",opts:["Dots.","Number code.","Letter code."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"This is a diagram of a.",opts:["Light Dependant Resistor.","Laser Diode Rectifier.","Logarithmic Differential Resistor."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"This is a diagram of a.",opts:["Variable Voltage source.","Variable Current source.","Variable Resistor."],ans:2,exp:"A potentiometer is not a voltage or current 'source'."},
    {topic:"",q:"Resistors required to carry a comparatively high current and dissipate high power are usually of.",opts:["Wire wound metal type.","Carbon compound type.","Wire wound ceramic type."],ans:2,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics by Eismin, Chapter 6, pg 101."},
    {topic:"",q:"A thyristor.",opts:["if energized on, will switch on a circuit.","has a positive temperature coefficient.","if energized on, will switch off a circuit."],ans:0,exp:"An SCR is an example of a thyristor."},
    {topic:"",q:"The temperature of a pure metal can greatly affect the resistance of it. What temperature scale is used?.",opts:["Absolute.","Centigrade.","Fahrenheit."],ans:1,exp:"08. Power."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 10 V battery supplies a resistive load of 10 ohms for 1 minute. What is the power supplied?.",opts:["100 W.","10 VA.","10 W."],ans:2,exp:"Power = I2R (time is not required). Resistive load only, so answer is in Watts."},
    {topic:"",q:"A resistor dissipates 80 Watts. If it runs for 4 hours 15 minutes, how much energy is dissipated in total?.",opts:["20.4 kJ.","1.224 MJ.","191.25 J."],ans:1,exp:"Energy = Power * Time. Time must be in seconds. There are 3600 seconds in 1 hour - not 60."},
    {topic:"",q:"The earth lead of a 24 V equipment is 0.5 ohm resistance and carries 80 A. How much power does it dissipate?.",opts:["1.92 kW.","1.152 kW.","3.2 kW."],ans:2,exp:"Power = I2R. Since it is an earth lead it will not have the full 24V on it, so disregard voltage."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the power dissipated in a 500 ohm resistor with a 3A flow?.",opts:["4.5 kW.","1.5 kW.","750 kW."],ans:0,exp:"Power = I2R."},
    {topic:"",q:"A DC circuit containing a total resistance of 100 ohms has a current flow of 250 mA. The power dissipated in the circuit is.",opts:["0.4 watts.","5 watts.","6.25 watts."],ans:2,exp:"Power = I2R."},
    {topic:"",q:"Power is the rate of doing work. It is measured in.",opts:["joules/second.","joules * seconds.","watts/second."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The power in a circuit when voltage and resistance is given can be found by.",opts:["V*R.","V*V*R.","V*V / R."],ans:1,exp:"Power = V 2 /R."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 500 ohm resistor carries a 3 amp current. What is the power dissipated?.",opts:["500 W.","5 kW.","4.5 kW."],ans:2,exp:"Power = I 2 R."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 48-volt source is required to furnish 192 watts to a parallel circuit consisting of three resistors of equal value. What is the value of each resistor?.",opts:["4 ohm.","36 ohm.","12 ohm."],ans:1,exp:"Each resistor dissipates 192/3 = 64 Watts. Voltage is the same across all resistors in parallel = 48 Volts. Power = V-squared/R. Work out R."},
    {topic:"",q:"A piece of equipment is rated at 750 watts and the applied voltage is 30 volts. The value of the protection fuse should be.",opts:["20 amps.","15 amps.","30 amps."],ans:2,exp:"Power = amps * volts. Find the amperage from that. Then choose the fuse of the nearest size up."},
    {topic:"",q:"How much power must a 24-volt generator furnish to a system which contains the following series loads? 5 ohm, 3 ohm and 12 ohm.",opts:["402 watts.","28.8 watts.","450 watts."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A direct current of 12 milliamperes flows through a circuit which has a resistance of 1000 ohms. The power dissipated by the circuit is.",opts:["144 milliwatts.","12 watts.","12 milliwatts."],ans:0,exp:"Power = I 2 R. Watch the prefix 'milli'. 'Milli' means divide by 1000. Square it and you must divide by 1,000,000."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a power circuit, the purpose of an inductor is.",opts:["to dampen voltage surges.","to dampen current surges.","to dampen power surges."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A single phase circuit has an input voltage of 100V. The current is 10A and the circuit has a power factor of 0.5. The true power is.",opts:["50W.","1000W.","500W."],ans:2,exp:"Apparent Power = IV = 100 * 10 = 1000 VA. True Power = PF * Apparent Power = 0.5 * 1000 = 500W."},
    {topic:"",q:"Power in a DC circuit is found by.",opts:["multiplying the voltage by itself and dividing by the current.","multiplying current by resistance.","multiplying the resistance by the current squared."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the energy supplied to a circuit for 4hrs 15 minutes at 800 j/sec what would be the energy consumed?.",opts:["3400 KW Hrs.","3.4 KW Hrs.","340 KW Hrs."],ans:1,exp:"4 hours 15 minutes = 4 1/4 hours. Energy = power * time = 800 * 4 1/4 = 3400 = 3.4 kW hrs."},
    {topic:"",q:"A heater which consumes 800 Kilojoules/sec is on for 4hrs 15min. The energy consumed is.",opts:["340 kWh.","3.4 kWh.","3400 kWh."],ans:2,exp:"kJ/s = kW. 800 * 4.25 = 3400 kWh."},
    {topic:"",q:"A voltage of 250V causes a current of 30mA. What is the wattage?.",opts:["7.5W.","7500W.","0.225W."],ans:0,exp:"Power = IV = 30/1000 * 250 = 7.5W."},
    {topic:"",q:"Which requires the most electrical power during operation?.",opts:["A 12-volt motor requiring 8 amperes.","Four 30-watt lamps in a 12-volt parallel circuit.","Two lights requiring 3 amperes each in a 24volt parallel system."],ans:2,exp:"a is 12*8=96W, b is 30*4 = 120W, c is 3*24*2=144W."},
    {topic:"",q:"The power dissipated in a circuit with a known potential difference and resistance is calculated by.",opts:["multiplying the square of the potential difference by the resistance.","dividing the square of the potential difference by the resistance.","finding the current and multiplying the answer by the resistance."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The power expended in a given circuit is.",opts:["inversely proportional to cube- root of the current.","proportional to the square-root of the voltage.","proportional to the square of the voltage."],ans:2,exp:"09. Capacitance/capacitor."},
    {topic:"",q:"A capacitor is fully charged after 25 seconds to a battery voltage of 20 Volts. The battery is replaced with a short circuit. What will be the voltage across the capacitor after one time constant?.",opts:["0 volts.","7.36 volts.","12.64 volts."],ans:1,exp:"The capacitor is discharging - so it discharges 63.2% in one time constant - so what is left?."},
    {topic:"",q:"The voltage rating of a capacitor is.",opts:["the max voltage that can be constantly applied.","the min voltage required to charge.","the normal operating voltage."],ans:0,exp:"Exceed the voltage at which the capacitor is rated, and you destroy the capacitor."},
    {topic:"",q:"The relative permittivity of a capacitor is.",opts:["the permittivity of the dielectric.","the relative permittivity of the dielectric in relation to a vacuum.","the permittivity of dielectric in relation to dry air."],ans:1,exp:"'Relative' means relative to 'free space' (a 'vacuum' in other words)."},
    {topic:"",q:"When checking a capacitor with an ohmmeter, if the reading shows charging but the final reading is less than normal then the possible fault is a.",opts:["leaking dielectric capacitor.","short circuit dielectric.","open circuit dielectric."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A capacitor with double the area and double the dielectric thickness will have.",opts:["double the capacitance.","the same capacitance.","half the capacitance."],ans:1,exp:"Get the formula for capacitance of a capacitor."},
    {topic:"",q:"An electrolytic capacitor is used where.",opts:["minimum losses are essential.","there is a polarized input.","high frequency AC is used."],ans:1,exp:"Wiring an electrolytic capacitor wrong polarity will destroy it. AC will therefore destroy it. Current must be DC (i.e. polarised) and the right way."},
    {topic:"",q:"What must you do to make a 3 F capacitor circuit into a 2 F circuit?.",opts:["Put a 2 F capacitor in parallel.","Put a 6 F capacitor in series.","Put a 1 F capacitor in series."],ans:1,exp:"To reduce the capacitance of a circuit you must place a capacitor in series (formula is same as resistors in parallel). You do the maths to see which one it is."},
    {topic:"",q:"The time required to fully charge a 1 μ(micro)F capacitor in series with a 100k ohm resistor is.",opts:["100 seconds.","100 milliseconds.","500 milliseconds."],ans:2,exp:"Time-constant (=RC) is 100 ms. Time-constant is time to reach 63.2% of full charge. Time to fully charge = 5 * TC = 500 ms."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 50 μ(micro) Farad capacitor is fed with a current of 25 milliAmpere. How long will it take to charge it to 100Volts?.",opts:["1 second.","0.2 seconds.","12 minutes."],ans:0,exp:"Work out the resistance in the circuit first (R=V/I) (4000 ohms). Then work out the time constant (TC=RC). Time to charge = 5RC."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a circuit C = 25 microfarads and the current flow is 40 microamps for 4 seconds. What is the voltage?.",opts:["6.4Volts.","3.2Volts.","12.8Volts."],ans:0,exp:"Since Q = VC and Q = It, then VC = It right? So V = It/C. Watch those unit prefixes."},
    {topic:"",q:"Three 12 μ(micro)F capacitors are in series. The total capacitance is.",opts:["12 μ(micro)F.","4 μ(micro)F.","36 μ(micro)F."],ans:1,exp:"Capacitors in series - total capacitance reduces. equal capacitors and total will be 1/3rd of one."},
    {topic:"",q:"The time constant of a capacitor is the time.",opts:["the current to reach 63.2% of maximum.","for the emf to reach 63.2% of maximum.","to reach maximum current."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The amount of electricity a capacitor can store is directly proportional to the.",opts:["plate area and inversely proportional to the distance between the plates.","distance between the plates and inversely proportional to the plate area.","plate area and is not affected by the distance between the plates."],ans:0,exp:"Get the formula for the capacitance of a capacitor."},
    {topic:"",q:"The capacitance of a capacitor is dependant upon.",opts:["the rate of change of current in the circuit.","the type of material separating the plates.","the charge on it."],ans:1,exp:"The capacitance of a capacitor is only dependant upon its physical properties (size and material) and not what you apply to it."},
    {topic:"",q:"When different rated capacitors are connected in parallel in a circuit, the total capacitance is.",opts:["less than the capacitance of the lowest rated capacitor.","equal to the capacitance of the highest rated capacitor.","equal to the sum of all the capacitances."],ans:2,exp:"Get the formula for capacitors in parallel (it is like resistors in series)."},
    {topic:"",q:"One Picofarad is.",opts:["1 * 10 12 farad.","1 * 10 -6 farad.","1 * 10 -12 farad."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The switch on a DC circuit containing a fully charged capacitor is opened. The voltage across the capacitor.",opts:["drops immediately to zero.","starts to fall exponentially to zero.","remains equal to the original charging voltage supply."],ans:2,exp:"If there is no external circuit, the charge can go nowhere."},
    {topic:"",q:"The current in a DC circuit containing a fully charged capacitor is.",opts:["zero.","is dependent upon the size of the capacitance of the capacitor.","maximum."],ans:0,exp:"If the capacitor is fully charged, it is equal and opposite to the source voltage, so no current flows."},
    {topic:"",q:"A capacitor is a barrier to.",opts:["both.","A.C.","D.C."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the total capacitance of a parallel circuit containing three capacitors with capacitance of 0.25microfarad, 0.03microfarad, and 0.12microfarad, respectively?.",opts:["0.4 μ(micro)F.","0.04 μ(micro)F.","0.04 piko Farad."],ans:0,exp:"Capacitors in parallel, the same formula as resistors in series - i.e. just add them all up."},
    {topic:"",q:"When two capacitors are connected in series.",opts:["the charge stored on each is inversely proportional to the voltage across it.","the charge stored on each is the same.","the charge stored on each is directly proportional to its capacitance."],ans:1,exp:"If they were not the same, there would be a current flow between them, until they equalised out."},
    {topic:"",q:"The charge on a capacitor is expressed as.",opts:["the ratio Q/V.","the product Q * V.","the product C * V."],ans:2,exp:"Remember Q = VC."},
    {topic:"",q:"When handling a high voltage capacitor in an electrical circuit, be sure it.",opts:["has a full charge before removing it from the circuit.","is fully discharged before removing it from the circuit.","has at least a residual charge before removing it from the circuit."],ans:1,exp:"Charged capacitors can be lethal, even when charged to say, 1 volt."},
    {topic:"",q:"The dielectric constant of a capacitor is a measurement of.",opts:["the electrical resistance of the capacitor dielectric.","the electrostatic energy storing capacity of the capacitor dielectric.","the electrical repulsion of electrons within the dielectric material."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Three 12 microfarad capacitors in parallel. The overall circuit capacitance is.",opts:["4 microfarads.","1 microfarads.","36 microfarads."],ans:2,exp:"Capacitors in parallel - just add them up."},
    {topic:"",q:"A circuit has 1 megohm and 8 microfarads. What is the time constant?.",opts:["8 seconds.","1 second.","40 seconds."],ans:0,exp:"Time constant = RC. The mega and the micro cancel."},
    {topic:"",q:"A capacitor rating is.",opts:["the voltage it will charge to.","the maximum continuous voltage it can take.","the voltage it will rupture at."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The multiplier colour coding on a capacitor is in.",opts:["picofarads.","farads.","microfarads."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When a capacitor is charged from a DC supply, the voltage/time curve is.",opts:["logarithmic.","linear.","exponential."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An electrolytic capacitor is used because it has a.",opts:["small physical size for low leakage current.","large physical size for a large capacity.","small physical size for a large capacity."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An electrolytic capacitor would be used in circuits supplying.",opts:["heavy loads.","light loads.","heavy / light loads."],ans:2,exp:"Electrolytic capacitors are used in circuits of all sizes."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 20 Picofarad capacitor with a 1 Megohm resistor takes how long to charge?.",opts:["100 microseconds.","20 milliseconds.","0.1 second."],ans:0,exp:"Time constant (TC) = RC = 20/1,000,000,000,000 * 1,000,000 = 20/1,000,000 = 20 microseconds. But fully charged is 5 * TC = 100 microseconds."},
    {topic:"",q:"The discharge curve of a capacitor is.",opts:["Exponential.","Logarithmic.","Linear."],ans:0,exp:"The capacitor discharge curve is an exponential decay, however, the charge curve is logarithmic (the inverse of exponential)."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the value of a monolithic capacitor with 103 on it?.",opts:["1000pf.","10,000pf.","100,000pf."],ans:1,exp:"103 means 10 followed by 3 zeros. Capacitors are always measured in picofarads."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the formula for working out the capacitance of a capacitor if K = dielectric constant, A = area of the plates, d = distance plates apart?.",opts:["c = K*A/d.","c = d/K*A.","c = K*d/A."],ans:0,exp:"C = K*A/d."},
    {topic:"",q:"Total capacitance of 3 capacitors each 12milliFarad in a parallel circuit.",opts:["36milliFarad.","12milliFarad.","6mf."],ans:0,exp:"Total C = = C1 + C2 + C3 (capacitors in parallel)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A combination of 1 megohm and 8 microfarad would give a time constant of.",opts:["40 seconds.","400 ms.","8 seconds."],ans:2,exp:"1,000,000 * 8/1,000,000 = 8 seconds. Note: Time Constant is asked for, not 'time to charge'."},
    {topic:"",q:"Generally, what is the voltage across a fully charged capacitor?.",opts:["Less than circuit voltage.","Same as circuit voltage.","More than circuit voltage."],ans:1,exp:"The answer to this question depends upon how accurate you want to go. Normal theory is that a fully charged capacitor has an equal (and opposite) voltage to the supply. However, a capacitor is never fully charged (something in the order of 99.999999% charged)."},
    {topic:"",q:"When would you use an electrolytic capacitor?.",opts:["On a balanced circuit.","Low output compared to size required.","Large output compared to size required."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A capacitor has Brown, Black and Orange bands. It's value is.",opts:["10 picofarads.","10 nanofarads.","100 picofarads."],ans:1,exp:"Brown = 1, Black = 0, Orange = x1000. Total is 10,000 picofarads (always picofarads with capacitors) = 10 nanofarads."},
    {topic:"",q:"Voltage measured between the plates of a capacitor will be generally.",opts:["can dangerously higher than applied voltage.","the same as applied voltage.","less than applied voltage."],ans:1,exp:"Technically the voltage on a charged capacitor never reaches the applied voltage. It gets to 99.99999% (and more)."},
    {topic:"",q:"Dielectric constant of a capacitor is.",opts:["max V that can be applied to a capacitor.","electrostatic storing capability of the dielectric.","max I stored in the capacitor."],ans:1,exp:"The dielectric stores electrical energy."},
    {topic:"",q:"One microfarad is.",opts:["1 * 10-12 farads.","1 * 1012 farads.","1 * 10-6 farads."],ans:2,exp:"Jepperson, A+P Technician General Handbook, Chapter 1, page 22."},
    {topic:"",q:"A circuit with a capacitor has 6 volts applied to it with a charge of 2400 pico-coulombs. What would the charge be if the applied voltage was 1.5 volts?.",opts:["2400 * 10-12 coulombs.","600 * 10-9 coulombs.","600 * 10-12 coulombs."],ans:2,exp:"Q = CV, so direct relationship of charge to voltage. 1.5V is 1/4 of 6V, so charge is 1/4 of 2400 picocoulombs. Note this is more of a question on pico and nano and whether you know the difference."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a capacitor, the dielectric strength is measured in.",opts:["Farads per meter.","Volts per metre.","Coulombs per m2."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Three capacitors connected in series, each having a value of 12 picofarads, calculate the total capacitance.",opts:["36 picofarads.","4 picofarads.","12 picofarads."],ans:1,exp:"12/3 = 4. 10a. Magnetism."},
    {topic:"",q:"Magnetic inclination is the least at the.",opts:["poles.","equator.","isoclines."],ans:1,exp:"'Inclination' is the same as 'dip'. Dip is greatest at the poles and least at the equator."},
    {topic:"",q:"Ferromagnetic materials can be magnetized.",opts:["below a certain temperature.","above a certain temperature.","within a band of temperatures."],ans:0,exp:"Read up on the 'Curie Point'."},
    {topic:"",q:"Glass is an example of a.",opts:["coercive material.","paramagnetic material.","diamagnetic material."],ans:2,exp:"Glass is diamagnetic."},
    {topic:"",q:"A solenoid of 10 turns per metre carries a current of 5A. If the current is reduced to 2.5A, how many turns would be required to maintain the same magnetic field?.",opts:["20.","50.","5."],ans:0,exp:"Flux = N*I, so halve the current you must double the windings."},
    {topic:"",q:"Which of the following materials is easiest to magnetize?.",opts:["High grade steel.","Soft iron.","Cast iron."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"To find which end of an electromagnet is the north pole, use the.",opts:["Fleming's Left Hand Rule.","Right Hand Clasp Rule.","Cork Screw Rule."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The earth's magnetic field is greatest at the.",opts:["magnetic equator.","geographic poles.","magnetic poles."],ans:2,exp:"Flux density is greatest at the magnetic poles, where all the flux lines converge."},
    {topic:"",q:"The lines of magnetic flux from a magnet will.",opts:["attract each other.","repel each other.","have no effect upon each other."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Of the following which pair of materials would most readily become magnetized?.",opts:["Iron and steel.","Copper and steel.","Nickel and bronze."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The core material used for an electromagnet is soft iron because.",opts:["its magnetism is not easily destroyed.","it retains most of its flux density when demagnetized.","it demagnetizes easily."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the North pole of a magnet is brought nearer to the North pole of another magnet.",opts:["attraction between them will be increased.","repulsion between them will be reduced.","repulsion between them will be increased."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Where is the magnetic dip least?.",opts:["Poles.","Isoclinals.","Equator."],ans:2,exp:"Magnetic dip is another name for inclination. It is least at the equator, and maximum (90 degrees)at the magnetic poles."},
    {topic:"",q:"When a number of ferrite pieces are grouped together, they.",opts:["are semi-permanent magnets when DC is passed through them.","electromagnets.","can be used to store binary code."],ans:2,exp:"Magnetic tape uses tiny ferrite particles coated onto a plastic tape."},
    {topic:"",q:"Ampere turns is calculated by the number of turns.",opts:["multiplied by current.","divided by current.","multiplied by magnetic flux."],ans:0,exp:"Ampere turns means AMPS * TURNS."},
    {topic:"",q:"The unit of flux is the.",opts:["Ampere turns / metre.","Weber.","Ampere turns."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Lines of magnetic flux pass from.",opts:["South to North.","East to West.","North to South."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Why is ferrite used in memory circuits?.",opts:["High reluctance.","Low permeability.","High remanence."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Magnetic lines are flowing parallel. They will.",opts:["have no effect on each other.","attract each other.","repel each other."],ans:2,exp:"Assuming this means parallel AND in the same direction, they will repel each other. Like the magnetic fields around two wires, carrying current in opposite directions."},
    {topic:"",q:"The horizontal component of the earth's magnetic field is most strongly felt at.",opts:["equator.","45 degrees latitude.","poles."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Two parallel lines of magnetic flux in the same polarity.",opts:["repel each other.","have no effect on each other.","attract each other."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Magnetic fields around two parallel conductors carrying current in the same directions will.",opts:["repel.","attract.","will attract or repel depending on the type of current."],ans:1,exp:"orcesfields/magneticforcesfields.html"},
    {topic:"",q:"Magnetic field on a solenoid is.",opts:["the same both inside and outside the coil.","weakest outside the coil.","strongest outside the coil."],ans:1,exp:"10b. Magnetism."},
    {topic:"",q:"Which of the following is absolute permeability?.",opts:["μ(micro)r.","μo(micro node).","μ(micro)."],ans:2,exp:"μ(micro) = μo(micro node)*μ(micro)r."},
    {topic:"",q:"The symbol for flux density is.",opts:["H(capital).","ø(phy).","B(capital)."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A paramagnetic material has a relative permeability of.",opts:["zero.","less than unity.","greater than unity."],ans:2,exp:"A paramagnetic material has a greater permeability than free space (vacuum). so its relative permeability is greater than 1."},
    {topic:"",q:"Through which material will magnetic lines of force pass the most readily?.",opts:["Copper.","Iron.","Aluminium."],ans:1,exp:"Iron (especially soft iron) has the greatest permeability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Permanent magnets have.",opts:["high reluctance, high coercive force.","low reluctance, high coercive force.","high reluctance, low coercive force."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The term used to denote the strength of a magnetic field is.",opts:["retentivity.","hysteresis.","flux density."],ans:2,exp:"Flux density is the number of flux lines per unit cross sectional area."},
    {topic:"",q:"A soft iron core is used in an ELECTRO magnet because.",opts:["it has LOW permeability and HIGH coercivity.","it has HIGH permeability and LOW coercivity.","it has HIGH permeability and HIGH coercivity."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A non-magnetic metal.",opts:["has high retentivity.","has no permeability.","is a poor conductor."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the effect of inserting an iron core into a current carrying coil?.",opts:["The flux density of the original magnetic field remains constant.","The core tends to move from a stronger to a weaker part of the field.","The flux density of the original magnetic field produced by the coil is increased."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Flux density will.",opts:["increase linearly with coercive force.","decrease linearly with magnetic flux.","increase linearly with magnetic flux."],ans:2,exp:"Flux density = magnetic flux lines per unit cross sectional area."},
    {topic:"",q:"Storage of magnets should be.",opts:["in a non magnetic pox.","in pairs with keeper plates.","in pairs end to end."],ans:1,exp:"Bar magnets are stored in pairs (end-to-end) and with keeper plates. (See forum for explanation)."},
    {topic:"",q:"The hysteresis loop for a magnetic material is on a graph with.",opts:["current against flux density.","flux density against magnetising force.","total flux against flux density."],ans:1,exp:"The hysteresis curve is MMF against B."},
    {topic:"",q:"A material with a narrow hysteresis loop.",opts:["cannot be magnetised.","will have high retentivity.","will have low retentivity."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If a bar magnet is cut in half.",opts:["the magnet is destroyed.","two bar magnets are formed.","one bar magnet and one non- magnet is formed."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Copper is a.",opts:["ferromagnetic material.","paramagnetic materials.","diamagnetic material."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The MMF of a coil fed with 2 amps and having 10 turns is.",opts:["5 ampere turns.","20 amperes / turn.","20 ampere turns."],ans:2,exp:"MMF (symbol H) is amps * turns. Unit is Ampere Turns."},
    {topic:"",q:"An example of a paramagnetic material is.",opts:["silver.","iron.","glass."],ans:1,exp:"Iron is a paramagnetic (ferromagnetic actually), the other 2 are diamagnetics (LBP notes are wrong about glass)."},
    {topic:"",q:"Vibration in a magnet causes.",opts:["flux to stay the same.","flux to decrease.","flux to increase."],ans:1,exp:"A good way to destroy a magnet is to drop it or subject it to high frequency vibrations."},
    {topic:"",q:"Permeability of a material can be found by.",opts:["flux density / MMF.","MMF * flux density.","MMF / flux density."],ans:0,exp:"Permeability = B/H (Reluctance = H/B, similar to resistance R = V/I)."},
    {topic:"",q:"When magnetizing a piece of material, magnetic strength will rise.",opts:["non-linearly with magnetic force.","linearly with coercive force.","linearly with magnetic force."],ans:0,exp:"Magnetic force is MMF (or H in Henries). It is not linear because of magnetic saturation."},
    {topic:"",q:"A material exposed to heat will.",opts:["have no effect on magnetism.","be easier to magnetise.","be harder to magnetise."],ans:2,exp:"The 'curie' temperature is the temperature above which the material cannot be magnetised."},
    {topic:"",q:"If cobalt has a permeability of 4800 it is a.",opts:["ferromagnetic.","paramagnetic.","diamagnetic."],ans:0,exp:"Cobalt is a hard ferromagnetic silver-white element."},
    {topic:"",q:"Cobalt has a permeability.",opts:["greater than unity.","less than unity.","same as unity."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Magnetic flux.",opts:["exist in all space around the magnet.","is more concentrated at the centre of bar magnet.","occupies the space around the magnet with equal flux density."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The ability of a circuit to produce a magnetic flux under the influence of a MMF.",opts:["permeance.","permeability.","permanence."],ans:1,exp:"Aircraft Electrical System. Pallett 3rd Edition appendix one page 196."},
    {topic:"",q:"Magnetic flux saturation takes place when.",opts:["the magnetised medium will accept no further lines of flux.","the magnetic field drops to zero.","the magnetic field starts to reduce with increased magnetising force."],ans:0,exp:"See a B-H diagram."},
    {topic:"",q:"Cables are shielded to prevent 'H' magnetic interference in wires with the following material.",opts:["Copper braiding.","Ferromagnetic material.","Diamagnetic material."],ans:0,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics, Eismin 5th edition Page 78."},
    {topic:"",q:"Spontaneous magnetism is associated with.",opts:["diamagnetic materials.","ferromagnetic materials.","paramagnetic materials."],ans:1,exp:"hg2m_b/hg2m_b.html."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two inductive coils are placed in close proximity with each other at 90 degrees. The number of flux linkages is.",opts:["0.","maximum negative.","maximum positive."],ans:0,exp:"The coils must be parallel for maximum flux linkages (coupling factor). Coupling factor reduces as angular difference increases to."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the mutual inductance if two coils of 10mH and 500mH have 90% of the flux transferred from one to the other?.",opts:["459mH.","4.5mH.","63mH."],ans:2,exp:"Total inductance = 0.9 * root * 10 * 500 = 63."},
    {topic:"",q:"An inductor has 1,000 turns of wire and a cross sectional area of 0.001m2. If the core has a permeability of 0.000,001 and the coil is 0.1m. What is the value of the inductor?.",opts:["100microH.","100mH.","10mH."],ans:2,exp:"0.000001*1000*0.001/0.1 = 0.01 = 10 mH."},
    {topic:"",q:"Three inductors 10 mH, 5 mH and 20 mH are connected in parallel. What is the total inductance?.",opts:["Without knowing the coupling factor the total inductance cannot be found.","2.86mH.","35mH."],ans:1,exp:"In parallel, the total inductance is lower than the lowest single inductance."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the current reaches a maximum through an inductor of 2A in 15 seconds, what is the current after 3 seconds?.",opts:["1.26A.","0.63A.","1A."],ans:0,exp:"1 time constant = 15/5 = 3s. 100% charge = 2A, so 1 time constant (63.2%) = 1.26A."},
    {topic:"",q:"The time constant of an inductor is.",opts:["L/R.","L 2 /R.","LR."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the rate of change of current is halved, mutual inductance will.",opts:["stay the same.","halve.","double."],ans:0,exp:"Get the formula for mutual inductance. Rate of change of current (i.e. frequency) is not in it."},
    {topic:"",q:"Why are the iron cores of most induction coils laminated?.",opts:["To reduce the effects of eddy currents.","To reduce the core reluctance.","To increase the core permeability."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When more than two inductors of different inductance are connected in parallel in a circuit, the total inductance is.",opts:["equal to the sum of the individual inductance.","equal to the inductance of the highest rated inductor.","less than the inductance of the lowest rated inductor."],ans:2,exp:"Adding inductors in parallel is the same formula as resistors in parallel (i.e. adding an inductor actually decreases the inductance)."},
    {topic:"",q:"The time constant in an inductive circuit is the time required for the current to reach.",opts:["70.7% of maximum value.","63.2% of maximum value.","63.7% of maximum value."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An AC inductive circuit has an inductance 0f 10 mH and a frequency input of 1000Hz. The opposition to current flow is approximately.",opts:["63 ohms.","63,000 ohms.","630 ohms."],ans:0,exp:"Inductive reactance is 2pi * f * L = 6.28 * 1000 * 10/1000 = 62.8 ohms."},
    {topic:"",q:"Rotating coil B through 90° will.",opts:["increase the EMF induced into B.","increase the EMF induced into A.","decrease the EMF induced into B."],ans:2,exp:"Coupling factor is maximum when the coils are parallel. It is zero if they are at 90° to each other."},
    {topic:"",q:"An inductor of resistance 5 kΩ and inductance 2mH is connected to a DC circuit. The time constant is.",opts:["400 ns.","10 seconds.","40 ms."],ans:0,exp:"Time constant = L/R = 2mH/5000 = 0.000,000,4 seconds = 400 ns."},
    {topic:"",q:"When switching off the supply, the back EMF in a collapsing field in an inductor.",opts:["can be multiple times bigger than forward EMF.","never exceeds forward EMF.","is equal to forward EMF."],ans:0,exp:"The size of the back-emf depends upon how fast the magnetic field is collapsing. That is how your car ignition turns 12V into several thousand volts."},
    {topic:"",q:"An induced current in a coil.",opts:["opposes the EMF producing it.","does not affect the EMF producing it.","aids the EMF producing it."],ans:0,exp:"The induced current in a coil (inductor) opposes the current which produces it."},
    {topic:"",q:"1 Volt is produced when a current of 1 Amp per second is varied in a coil'. This is a definition of.",opts:["1 Ampere per metre.","1 Henry.","1 Coulomb per second."],ans:1,exp:"Lens' Law BEMF = -L dI/dt Aircraft Electrical Systems Pallet's 3rd Edition Appendix 1 page 195."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two inductor coils in very close proximity; if one of the coils is rotated 90 degrees slowly away from the other the mutual inductance.",opts:["decreases.","remains the same.","increases."],ans:0,exp:"Coupling factor (mutual inductance) decreases to zero at 90 degrees."},
    {topic:"",q:"An inductor is said to be saturated when.",opts:["the current is at a maximum.","the current through it is zero.","it will no longer accept lines of flux."],ans:2,exp:"12. DC Motor/Generator Theory."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a DC motor, the stator's magnetic field, due to armature reaction, moves.",opts:["in the same direction of rotation of the armature.","in the opposite direction of rotation of the armature.","into alignment with the GNA."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A small air gap between magnetic poles results.",opts:["in a weaker field than a large air gap, for the same magnetising force.","in a stronger field than a large air gap, for the same magnetising force.","in the same field as a large air gap, for the same magnetising force."],ans:2,exp:"Magnetic field (number of flux lines) is the same regardless of distance between poles. However, the field density (flux density) will increase with a smaller air gap."},
    {topic:"",q:"The electromagnetic brake coil in an actuator would be energised.",opts:["only at the instant of starting and stopping.","all the time in flight.","only when the actuator is running."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a shunt wound direct current motor with a constant voltage field supply, the torque developed by the motor is.",opts:["independent of load.","directly proportional to armature current.","inversely proportional to the armature current."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Decreasing the field current in a shunt motor will.",opts:["decrease speed and increase torque.","increase speed and increase torque.","increase speed and decrease torque."],ans:1,exp:"Decreasing the field current in a shunt motor will reduce the back emf in the armature so the armature current will increase giving a rise in torque and the RPM will increase as a consequence."},
    {topic:"",q:"To calculate generator output you need to know the.",opts:["armature speed and number of series conductors.","armature speed and field strength.","armature speed and number of parallel conductors."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a shunt motor, if you reverse both field current and the armature current, the motor will.",opts:["stop.","change direction.","continue to run in the same direction."],ans:2,exp:"Prove it with Fleming's left hand rule."},
    {topic:"",q:"A starter generator has a.",opts:["low resistance series field and a low resistance shunt field.","low resistance series field and a high resistance shunt field.","high resistance series field and a low resistance shunt field."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the brake coil on an actuator motor goes open circuit, the actuator will.",opts:["run slower.","stop.","over-ride its mechanical stops."],ans:1,exp:"The brake coil is wired in series with the actuator motor coil. It withdraws a spring loaded lock to release the actuator when current is applied."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a generator speed is halved and the field strength is doubled, the voltage output will be.",opts:["unchanged.","doubled.","halved."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"How can the direction of rotation of a DC electric motor be changed?.",opts:["reverse the electrical connections to either the field or armature windings.","rotate the positive brush one commutator segment.","interchange the wires which connect the motor to the external power source."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The only practical method of maintaining a constant voltage output from an aircraft generator under varying conditions of speed and load is to vary the.",opts:["speed at which the armature rotates.","strength of the magnetic field.","number of conductors in the armature."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Electric motors are often classified according to the method of connecting the field coils and armature. Aircraft",opts:["Compound.","Shunt (parallel).","Series."],ans:2,exp:"Series motors have the highest starting torque."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a heavy mechanical load is removed from a series motor.",opts:["the speed will increase and the armature current will increase.","the speed will increase and the armature current will decrease.","the speed will decrease and the armature current will decrease."],ans:1,exp:"Speed increases and increases the back emf which decreases the armature current."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the correct supply were connected to a DC shunt motor which had lost its residual magnetism it would.",opts:["run in the reverse direction.","fail to run.","run normally."],ans:2,exp:"Its a motor, not a generator!."},
    {topic:"",q:"One purpose of a growler test is to determine the presence of:.",opts:["a shorted armature.","a broken field lead.","an out-of-round commutator."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The purpose of an end-travel microswitch in a linear actuator is to.",opts:["remain open and closes at end travel only.","remain open during normal operation and only close if the actuator overruns its stops.","remain closed and opens at end travel only."],ans:2,exp:"See Pallett - Aircraft Electrical Systems, pg 140."},
    {topic:"",q:"Linear actuators used in aircraft are of.",opts:["split field series wound type.","compound wound type.","split field shunt wound type."],ans:0,exp:"See Pallett - Aircraft Electrical Systems, pg 141."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a generator sparks, a possible reason is.",opts:["the brush springs are loose.","magnetic flux deflecting the EMF.","the brushes have been placed around the magnetic coil."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the field strength of a generator is doubled, and the speed is doubled, the output EMF will.",opts:["stay the same.","double.","quadruple."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a DC motor the pole pairs are.",opts:["connected to the brush gear.","part of the armature.","embedded within the field coils."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A series wound DC motor, the field has.",opts:["few turns of thin wire.","many turns of thin wire.","few turns of thick wire."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Doubling the running time of an electrical machine would.",opts:["double the current used.","double the joules used.","double the watts used."],ans:1,exp:"Power (watts) is the rate of using energy (Joules). So doubling running time will not change the power. Current is the rate of charge flow - so more charge (coulombs) will be used but not at a faster or slower 'rate'."},
    {topic:"",q:"On a linear actuator, the field cutoff coil will be energized when.",opts:["only as the actuator commences movement.","the actuator is not running.","the actuator is running."],ans:2,exp:"Field cutoff coil is believed to be the brake coil."},
    {topic:"",q:"An electric motor produces a force of 5 N at a distance of 0.2m from the centre of rotation and rotates at a speed of 100 revs/sec. The motors output is.",opts:["100 horsepower.","628.4 watts.","314.2 watts."],ans:1,exp:"Power in a motor = torque * 2pi * revs/sec. Torque = force * radius."},
    {topic:"",q:"Starter motors are usually.",opts:["series wound.","compound wound.","shunt wound."],ans:0,exp:"Series wound motors have the greatest starting torque."},
    {topic:"",q:"Generator brushes are normally made of.",opts:["steel.","carbon.","brass."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"As the generator load is increased (within its rated capacity), the voltage will.",opts:["remain constant and the amperage output will decrease.","decrease and the amperage output will increase.","remain constant and the amperage output will increase."],ans:2,exp:"The voltage regulator keeps the generator voltage output the same. Current will increase as the load resistance drops."},
    {topic:"",q:"The voltage output of a generator is controlled by.",opts:["varying the current of the output.","varying the resistance of the output.","varying the current of the field."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The current flowing in the armature of a DC motor is equal to.",opts:["(applied volts-generated volts) / armature resistance.","(applied volts-generated volts) * armature resistance.","applied volts + generated volts / armature resistance."],ans:0,exp:"Ohms law I = V/R. Voltage in the armature is the applied voltage - back emf (generated voltage)."},
    {topic:"",q:"What device is used to convert alternating current, which has been induced into the loops of the rotating armature of a DC generator into direct current as it leaves the generator?.",opts:["An inverter.","A commutator.","A rectifier."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Which of the following is not one of the purposes of interpoles in a generator?.",opts:["Overcome armature reaction.","Reduce arcing at the brushes.","Reduce field strength."],ans:2,exp:"NOT' one of the purposes. The purpose of interpoles is to overcome (in fact 'prevent') armature reaction. Armature reaction is partially responsible for arcing at the brushes."},
    {topic:"",q:"To reverse the direction of a series motor.",opts:["the direction of the current through the field and the armature is reversed.","the direction of the current through the field is reversed.","a second series field is fitted."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The current consumed by a DC starter motor will.",opts:["remain relatively constant over the starting speed range.","decrease as the engine speed increases.","increase as the engine speed increases."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"How are generators rated?.",opts:["Impedance at rated voltage.","Amperes at rated voltage.","Watts at rated voltage."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Due to armature reaction in a DC motor.",opts:["the leading pole tips are magnetically weakened.","the trailing pole tips are magnetically weakened.","the magnetic flux is restored by moving the MNA towards the GNA."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Some electric motors have two sets of field winding wound in opposite directions so that the.",opts:["speed of the motor can be more closely controlled.","motor can be operated in either direction.","power output of the motor can be more closely controlled."],ans:1,exp:"Specifically series motors."},
    {topic:"",q:"What polarity do interpoles take in a DC motor?.",opts:["Either, provided they lay on the MNA.","The same as the next main pole ahead in the direction of rotation.","The same as the next main pole behind in the direction of rotation."],ans:2,exp:"The interpole must pull back the magnetic field. In a motor, the magnetic field deflects in the opposite direction to the armature."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the principal advantage of the series-wound DC motor?.",opts:["Suitable for constant speed use.","High starting torque.","Low starting torque."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Interpole windings fitted to DC series wound generators are.",opts:["in series with the armature.","in series with the field.","in parallel with the armature."],ans:0,exp:"Interpole windings are always in series with the armature, so they carry the same current as the armature."},
    {topic:"",q:"What polarity do interpoles take in a DC generator?.",opts:["the same as the next main pole behind in the direction of rotation.","either, providing they lay on M.N.A.","the same as the next main pole ahead in the direction of rotation."],ans:0,exp:"On a generator, the field is deflected in the same direction as the armature rotates. The interpoles must drag back the field."},
    {topic:"",q:"The method most often used in overcoming the effect of armature reaction is through the use of.",opts:["shaded poles.","interpoles.","drum-wound armatures in combination with a negatively connected series field."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The starting current of a series-wound DC motor, in passing through both the field and armature windings produces a.",opts:["speed slightly higher when unloaded.","high starting torque.","low starting torque."],ans:1,exp:"At low speed (i.e starting) there is a very high current in the armature (due to no back-emf). Since the field is in series with the armature, the same high current flows in it, resulting in a very high torque."},
    {topic:"",q:"To increase the speed of a shunt motor a resistance is placed.",opts:["in parallel with the field.","in series with the field.","in series with the armature."],ans:1,exp:"An apparent anomaly. Increasing the field resistance decreases the field strength which reduces the back-emf in the armature. Armature current therefore increases and its speed increases."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 200 volt motor is taking 10 amperes armature current, the armature resistance is 0.1 ohm. The Back-EMF under these conditions will be.",opts:["201 volts.","199 volts.","1 volt."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is a method used for restoring generator field residual magnetism?.",opts:["Energize the armature.","Flash the fields.","Reseat the brushes."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A high surge of current is required when a DC electric motor is first started. As the speed of the motor increase.",opts:["the counter emf decreases proportionally.","the applied emf increases proportionally.","the counter emf builds up and opposes the applied emf, thus reducing the current flow through the armature."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Aircraft generators are cooled by.",opts:["fuel cooling radiators.","oil cooling radiators around the main body.","ram air."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"On a combined DC starter - generator system.",opts:["the voltage regulator is connected to the shunt field after the start cycle is completed.","the voltage regulator controls the start sequence during engine starting.","the series coil is open circuit during the engine start sequence."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the field current to a shunt wound DC motor is decreased when operating at a constant load, the motor speed will.",opts:["reduce.","not change.","increase."],ans:2,exp:"This may seem strange, but decreasing the field strength of a shunt motor will decrease the back emf of the armature and hence increase the current through the armature. This in turn increases the armature speed."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a motor is spinning at 50 cycles per second, how long is 1 cycle of the output?.",opts:["50 seconds.","0.83 seconds.","0.02 seconds."],ans:2,exp:"Time period = 1/frequency."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a an DC motor, the rotation of the MNA.",opts:["is opposite to the rotation of the armature.","is slightly less speed than the rotation of the armature.","is the same as the rotation of the armature."],ans:0,exp:"The field in a motor reacts to the armature current by 'distorting' in the opposite direction to the armature - called armature reaction."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a wave wound generator with 4 poles, what would be the minimum number of brushes?.",opts:["4.","2.","8."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A wire is rotated through a magnetic field. To give DC it must be connected to.",opts:["a commutator.","slip rings.","a rectifier."],ans:0,exp:"A commutator turns AC to DC in a generator."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the supply to a DC shunt motor was reversed.",opts:["it would rotate in the same direction.","it would fail to run.","it would rotate in the opposite direction."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A series motor draws a high current on start, and then the current reduces. This is due to.",opts:["the resistance of the field and the armature in series.","the resistance of the field and armature in parallel.","the back EMF."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Interpoles in a DC generator are connected.",opts:["in series with the armature.","in series with the field.","in parallel with the armature."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a DC generator, the effect of armature reaction may be reduced by moving the brush gear.",opts:["in the direction of armature rotation towards the MNA.","from MNA to GNA.","against the direction of armature rotation towards the MNA."],ans:0,exp:"The MNA leads the GNS in a generator. The brushes should be put on the MNA."},
    {topic:"",q:"The windings embedded in the pole faces of a DC generator are.",opts:["commutating coils.","interpoles.","compensating windings."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A 6 pole wave- wound generator has.",opts:["3 brushes.","2 brushes.","6 brushes."],ans:1,exp:"A wave-wound generator needs only 2 brushes regardless of the number of poles, but some may have more for increased efficiency."},
    {topic:"",q:"Armature reaction in a DC generator causes.",opts:["MNA to move in the opposite direction of rotation.","MNA to move in the direction of rotation.","GNA to move in the direction of rotation."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a DC motor, back-EMF rises if the motor.",opts:["speed falls.","speed faload rises.","speed rises."],ans:2,exp:"B-EMF is due to generator action in the motor."},
    {topic:"",q:"A DC generator armature has 6 poles and is lap wound. The number of brushes required are.",opts:["2.","3.","6."],ans:2,exp:"6 poles is 3 pairs. Lap wound generators require 1 pair of brushes for each pair of poles."},
    {topic:"",q:"Reactive sparking is reduced by.",opts:["interpoles.","compensating windings.","auxiliary windings."],ans:0,exp:"Interpoles 'reduce' armature reaction (which causes reactive sparking). Compensating windings 'prevent' armature reaction."},
    {topic:"",q:"The back-EMF in a DC motor is.",opts:["equal to the applied EMF.","less than the applied EMF.","greater than the applied EMF."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The flux density of a magnetic field is 1.5T. The length of the conductor in the field is 2 m and the current flowing through the conductor is 5 amps. The force on the conductor is.",opts:["0.6 Newtons.","15 Newtons.","3.75 Newtons."],ans:1,exp:"F = BIl = 1.5 * 5 * 2 = 15N."},
    {topic:"",q:"A DC shunt motor has 28 volts applied. The current taken from the armature of 2 ohms resistance is 1 amp. The Back- EMF is.",opts:["26 V.","30 V.","27 V."],ans:0,exp:"Total V = I * R = 1 * 2 = 2V. So 26V B-EMF must be generated by the generator action."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a motor, armature reaction causes the MNA to move.",opts:["against the direction of rotation.","in the direction of rotation.","to the GNA."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a generator system, a stabilizing winding is used.",opts:["in series with the field to prevent oscillations.","to control output current.","to prevent voltage overshoot."],ans:2,exp:"See CAIP EEL/1-2. The stabilizing winding picks up a 'rate of change' from the output of an AC brushless generator and damps out voltage overshoot and prevents system oscillation. The pickup is an induction coil wound around the output so is NOT wired in series."},
    {topic:"",q:"What part of a DC motor would you find the poles?.",opts:["brushes.","rotor.","stator."],ans:2,exp:"The poles are a part of the field - which is the stator on a DC motor."},
    {topic:"",q:"The main advantage of a starter-generator over conventional units is.",opts:["it can produce a greater power output.","for the same power output there is a weight reduction and the starter drive remains engaged.","for the same power output there is a weight reduction and the drive is constantly engaged."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An A.C. shunt wound motor sometimes uses a volts dropping resistor. The resistor would be.",opts:["in series with the field.","in series with the supply.","in series with the armature."],ans:1,exp:"Must be referring to a universal motor (AC shunt wound?) The volts dropping resistor is placed in series with the supply."},
    {topic:"",q:"Commutator ripple can be reduced by.",opts:["increasing generator frequency.","the use of a ripple filter.","a resistor in series with the armature."],ans:1,exp:"A ripple filter is made of a capacitor in parallel with the generator output and an inductor in series with the generator output."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a DC motor the interpoles are part of the.",opts:["rotor assembly.","stator assembly.","field."],ans:2,exp:"Interpoles are a part of the Field. Rotor and Stator are terms for AC motors."},
    {topic:"",q:"Armature reaction is.",opts:["the MMF opposing rotation.","due to dirty or worn commutator.","reactive sparking."],ans:0,exp:"The MMF is the ampere-turns of the armature, producing a magnetic field in the armature, which opposes the main field and causes it to distort (in direction of motion of the armature)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A shunt motor.",opts:["is constant speed.","has high starting torque.","gives constant torque with variations in speed."],ans:0,exp:"DC Shunt motors are 'constant speed' motors, but starting torque is small."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a DC shunt motor, if the field resistance is increased what happens to the back EMF?.",opts:["decreases.","remains the same.","increases."],ans:0,exp:"The back EMF decreases with an increase in the field resistance. The armature current would increase and the motor speed would increase."},
    {topic:"",q:"To increase the voltage output of a generator you can.",opts:["decrease speed.","It is not speed dependant.","increase speed."],ans:2,exp:"Increasing the speed makes the armature cut the magnetic field faster, inducing a greater voltage."},
    {topic:"",q:"Generator voltage is.",opts:["dependant on speed of rotation.","independent of speed of rotation.","not dependant on speed of rotation."],ans:0,exp:"The faster the generator turns, the more voltage it produces. Besides, 'Independent' and 'not dependant' mean the same thing."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the main disadvantage of a starter generator?.",opts:["Heavier than like for like.","Will not supply output at low rpm.","Can only be used on piston engines."],ans:1,exp:"At low RPM, starter-generator is still in motor configuration."},
    {topic:"",q:"Back-EMF is the greatest when a motor is.",opts:["at operating speed.","under heavy load.","just starting to rotate."],ans:0,exp:"Back EMF is greatest when motor is at high speed."},
    {topic:"",q:"When a conductor carrying a current and placed in a magnetic field it experiences.",opts:["an electrostatic force.","an electromagnetic force.","a magnetic force."],ans:1,exp:"The force is generated by interaction of two magnetic fields."},
    {topic:"",q:"The type of motor used in a linear actuator is.",opts:["parallel (shunt wound).","compound.","series."],ans:2,exp:"Aircraft Electrical Systems Pallett Page 140."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the speed of a coil in a magnetic field is doubled and the field strength is doubled the output will.",opts:["quadruple.","stay the same.","halve."],ans:0,exp:"Voltage = Blv. Double B (field strength) and double v (velocity) and Voltage will quadruple."},
    {topic:"",q:"How many brushes are used in an 8 pole wave wound machine.",opts:["6.","8.","2."],ans:2,exp:"Any wave wound DC generator usually has only 1 pair of brushes."},
    {topic:"",q:"In the shunt generator the field is supplied by.",opts:["the load.","the armature.","a separate supply."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Increasing the speed of an aircraft generator results in an automatic.",opts:["field circuit resistance decrease.","armature circuit resistance increase.","field circuit resistance increase."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Which generators are usually used on aircraft?.",opts:["Shunt.","Compound.","Series."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a cumulative compound wound generator the.",opts:["the series and shunt fields assist each other.","the series and shunt fields oppose each other.","the voltage falls suddenly with an increase in load."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The shunt wound generator is controlled by a variable resistance in.",opts:["parallel with the field.","series with the armature.","series with the field."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The series wound generator's terminal voltage will, when below saturation.",opts:["increase with an increase in load.","decrease with an increase in load.","decrease with a decrease in rotational speed."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The shunt wound generator is normally started.",opts:["on load.","Either of the above.","off load."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"How do you reduce ripple on the output of a DC generator?.",opts:["Increase the speed of the armature and add more turns.","Decrease the speed of the armature and add more series windings.","Increase the commutator segments and connect each to a separate parallel winding."],ans:1,exp:"This amounts to increasing the separate armature windings which will increase the ripple frequency but decrease their amplitude."},
    {topic:"",q:"Armature reaction in a DC motor causes the neutral axis to shift in.",opts:["the direction in the direction of armature rotation.","the direction opposite to the direction of armature rotation.","either direction depending on current flow."],ans:1,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics by Eismin, Chapter 10, pg 194."},
    {topic:"",q:"An indication of good commutation on a DC machine would be.",opts:["high armature currents.","little or no sparking at the commutator.","silent operation of the machine."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If compound DC generators are operated in parallel they must.",opts:["all rotate at the same speed.","have an equalising or load sharing loop.","all use the same voltage."],ans:1,exp:"Eismin - Aircraft Electricity & Electronics - page 199."},
    {topic:"",q:"The windings embedded in the pole faces of a generator are.",opts:["commutating coils.","interpoles.","compensating coils."],ans:2,exp:"Eismin - Aircraft Electricity & Electronics - page 195."},
    {topic:"",q:"Prolonged reactive sparking in a DC generator could damage the.",opts:["armature.","commutator.","pole pieces."],ans:1,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics Eismin Page 197."},
    {topic:"",q:"The poles of a DC generator are laminated to.",opts:["reduce eddy current losses.","reduce flux losses.","reduce hysteresis losses."],ans:2,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics Eismin Page 186."},
    {topic:"",q:"One advantage of a DC motor over an AC motor is.",opts:["the direction of rotation of the rotor can be changed.","less voltage is required in DC than AC.","the DC motor will use less current."],ans:2,exp:"Due to the generated back emf, a DC motor will use less current."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a shunt wound direct current motor the torque is.",opts:["inversely proportional to the current in the armature.","independent of load.","proportional to the current in the armature."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If you apply this waveform to this circuit, what is the output across the capacitor?.",opts:["Pulsed negative.","Pulsed positive.","Zero."],ans:1,exp:"With the output taken across the capacitor, this is a basic integrator - so the output is."},
    {topic:"",q:"What value is the same as the equivalent D.C. heating effect?.",opts:["Peak.","Average.","Root Mean Square."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What shape is the waveform when the input pulse and the time base are unequal?.",opts:["Saw tooth.","Square.","Rectangular."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A sine wave has 5 amps RMS value. What is the peak value?.",opts:["7.07 amps.","6.37 amps.","10 amps."],ans:0,exp:"Peak = 1.414 * RMS (also remember that 5 is half of 10). 13. AC Theory."},
    {topic:"",q:"Convention requires that in a symmetrical 3 phase system, the.",opts:["red voltage is taken as the reference phase.","yellow voltage phase leads the red phase by 120 degrees.","red voltage phase leads the blue phase by 120 degrees."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the phase difference in a circuit with 100V, drawing 0.5 amps, consuming 50 Watts?.",opts:["0°.","45°.","90°."],ans:0,exp:"When power is in Watts, it is all true power - there is no apparent power."},
    {topic:"",q:"The power factor of a circuit containing an imbalance of inductive and capacitive reactance is.",opts:["greater than unity.","unity.","less than unity."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the wave shape of the x-axis input of a traversing oscilloscope image?.",opts:["Rectangular.","Triangular.","Square."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a purely resistive AC circuit, the current vector is.",opts:["+90° out of phase with the voltage vector.","in phase with the voltage vector.","-90° out of phase with the voltage vector."],ans:1,exp:"Purely resistive - current and voltage must be in phase."},
    {topic:"",q:"When comparing the average values of an AC generator output, to the peak values.",opts:["average voltage = 0.707 * peak voltage.","average voltage = 0.63 * peak voltage.","average voltage = same as the peak voltage."],ans:1,exp:"Don't get confused with RMS value."},
    {topic:"",q:"A parallel circuit at resonance has.",opts:["maximum impedance.","zero impedance.","minimum impedance."],ans:0,exp:"A 'series' circuit at resonance has zero impedance. A 'parallel' circuit at resonance has maximum impedance."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 10 ohm resistor has a 14.14 V peak drop across it. What power is dissipated?.",opts:["10 W.","19.99 W.","1.414 W."],ans:0,exp:"Since it mentions peak, it is AC. Always use RMS voltage when calculating power. RMS voltage = peak voltage / 1.414 (more than coincidence that 14.14 / 1.414 = 10). Power = V2/ R."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a 1 ohm circuit produces a 50 W output, the phase angle is.",opts:["45°.","0°.","90°."],ans:1,exp:"Going by the power being quoted in Watts (rather than VA) the circuit is purely resistive and therefore the phase angle is 0 degrees."},
    {topic:"",q:"What does a rectifier do?.",opts:["Changes alternating current into direct current.","Reduces voltage.","Changes direct current into alternating current."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The ratio between apparent power and true power is the.",opts:["power rating.","power factor.","efficiency."],ans:1,exp:"Power factor = true power / apparent power."},
    {topic:"",q:"One advantage of using AC electrical power in aircraft is.",opts:["the greater ease in stepping the voltage up or down.","AC electrical motors can be reversed while DC motors cannot.","the effective voltage is 1.41 times the maximum instantaneous voltage; therefore, less power input is required."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Unless otherwise specified, any values given for current or voltage in an AC circuit are assumed to be.",opts:["effective values.","instantaneous values.","maximum values."],ans:0,exp:"Effective values' is another term for RMS because it is the same heating 'effect' as DC."},
    {topic:"",q:"Which aircraft circuit would be most likely to use frequency wild 200V AC?.",opts:["Hydraulic pump.","Windscreen heating.","Standby compass."],ans:1,exp:"Frequency wild can only be used on resistive circuits (such as heating and lighting)."},
    {topic:"",q:"In an AC circuit how is the value of true power calculated?.",opts:["By voltmeter readings multiplied by ammeter readings.","By volts multiplied by amps multiplied by power factor.","By watt meter readings multiplied by power factor."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"One of the chief advantages of alternating current is that it can be transmitted at a high voltage with a low power loss; the voltage can then be changed to any desired value of.",opts:["DC by means of transformers.","DC by means of inverters.","AC by means of transformers."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"How many amperes will a 28-volt generator be required to supply to a circuit containing five lamps in parallel, three of which have a resistance of 6 ohms each and two of which have a resistance of 5 ohms each?.",opts:["1 ampere.","25.23 amperes.","1.11 amperes."],ans:1,exp:"resistance, then use ohms law (I=V/R) to find the total current. It is a tricky calculation to do without a calculator, so round the total resistance up (to 1 ohms) then choose the nearest answer for I."},
    {topic:"",q:"Frequency (Hz) is the number of cycles per.",opts:["minute.","second.","revolution."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The frequency of a power wave in an AC resistance circuit is.",opts:["half the frequency for voltage and current.","the same as the frequency for voltage and current.","twice the frequency for voltage and current."],ans:2,exp:"Look at a diagram of a power wave."},
    {topic:"",q:"In an AC circuit, the effective voltage is.",opts:["less than the maximum instantaneous voltage.","equal to the maximum instantaneous voltage.","greater than the maximum instantaneous voltage."],ans:0,exp:"Effective voltage is another term for RMS voltage, due to it being equal to the DC heating effect."},
    {topic:"",q:"Which wave form is stepped positive and negative with unequal length sides?.",opts:["Rectangular wave.","Trapezoidal wave.","Saw tooth wave."],ans:1,exp:"A trapezoidal waveform ALWAYS has unequal (vertical) sides. However, a sawtooth wave could also be considered to have unequal length sides (but does not necessarily have)."},
    {topic:"",q:"The value of an AC sine wave that will give an equivalent heating effect in a DC resistor is the.",opts:["peak value.","RMS value.","average value."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The peak factor for a sine wave is.",opts:["1.11.","0.707.","1.414."],ans:2,exp:"Peak factor (sometimes called 'crest factor') is calculated by peak value/RMS value. For a sine wave it is 1.414."},
    {topic:"",q:"An integrated step input at unequal time base produces.",opts:["a sine wave.","a square wave.","a saw tooth wave."],ans:2,exp:"Integrating a step input produces a 'ramp' or sawtooth waveform."},
    {topic:"",q:"The mark-to-space ratio of the waveform shown is.",opts:["A to D / D to E.","D to E / D to C.","A to B / B to C."],ans:0,exp:"Mark-to-space is the ratio of the duration of the square wave's positive amplitude (represented by a mark) to its negative amplitude (represented by a space)."},
    {topic:"",q:"Form Factor for a sine wave AC output is.",opts:["1.1.","1.414.","0.707."],ans:0,exp:"Form Factor = RMS / Average = 0.707 / 0.63 (for a sine wave)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A sine wave of RMS value 7.07 volts has a peak to peak value of.",opts:["20.0 volts.","10.0 volts.","0.707 volts."],ans:0,exp:"Peak = RMS * 1.414 7.07 * 1.414 = 10V Peak-to-peak = 2 * peak = 20V."},
    {topic:"",q:"The true power in an AC circuit is given by.",opts:["volts * amps.","PF * volts * amps.","voltmeter reading * PF."],ans:1,exp:"True power = PF * Apparent power. Apparent power = volts * amps."},
    {topic:"",q:"The average value of 100 volts peak AC is.",opts:["70.7 volts.","141.4 volts.","63.7 volts."],ans:2,exp:"Average value of a sine wave is 0.637 * peak."},
    {topic:"",q:"The RMS value of 200 volts peak to peak is.",opts:["127.4 volts.","70.7 volts.","141.4 volts."],ans:2,exp:"RMS = 0.707 * peak. Peak is 1/2 of peak-to-peak."},
    {topic:"",q:"The power factor of an AC circuit is the.",opts:["cosine of the phase angle.","tangent of the phase angle.","sine of the phase angle."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The Form Factor of an AC waveform can be found by.",opts:["RMS value divided by the average value.","average value divided by the RMS value.","average value times the RMS value."],ans:0,exp:"Form Factor = RMS / Average."},
    {topic:"",q:"A differentiator has a time constant which is.",opts:["long.","equal to the input pulse.","short."],ans:2,exp:"A differentiator provides a voltage proportional to the rate of change of the input. So for a square wave input, it needs to give a pulse on the step up, nothing on the straight bit of the square wave, then a negative pulse as the input drops back to zero. Only a very short time constant can do this."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the frequency is increased in an AC circuit of pure resistance, the effect is.",opts:["nothing.","decreased resistance.","increased resistance."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What sort of wave would be used in a CR oscilloscope to control sweep?.",opts:["Square.","Sine wave.","Sawtooth."],ans:2,exp:"A sawtooth wave is used in an oscilloscope to control sweep."},
    {topic:"",q:"If an AC sine wave has an RMS value of 5V its peak value is.",opts:["6.37 V.","7.07 V.","14.14 V."],ans:1,exp:"Peak value = 1.414 * RMS = 1.414 * 5 = 7.07 V."},
    {topic:"",q:"Apparent power in an AC circuit is found by.",opts:["V * I * cosØ.","V * I * sinØ.","V * I."],ans:2,exp:"Apparent power = amps * volts."},
    {topic:"",q:"A sine wave has a periodic time of 0.5 milliseconds, what is its frequency?.",opts:["200 Hz.","2Khz.","20Khz."],ans:1,exp:"Frequency is 1/time period. Watch that prefix 'milli'. f = 1/T = 1/0.0005 = 10,000/5 = 2000 Hz = 2 kHz."},
    {topic:"",q:"Phase angle is.",opts:["the cosine of the peak value of an ac voltage.","the difference in degrees of rotation between 2 alternating voltages or currents or a voltage and a current.","the difference in degrees between the positive and negative parts of an AC sine wave."],ans:1,exp:"Phase angle is the difference in degrees of rotation between 2 alternating voltages or currents or a voltage and a current."},
    {topic:"",q:"The impedance of an AC circuit is measured in.",opts:["ohms.","kilovolt-amperes.","amperes."],ans:0,exp:"Impedance is 'resistance to electron flow' in an AC circuit and is measured in Ohms."},
    {topic:"",q:"What happens to the current in a series resistive resonant circuit?.",opts:["It is maximum.","It is minimum.","It is zero."],ans:0,exp:"At resonance the impedance is minimum (zero apart from the resistance in the circuit) and so current is highest."},
    {topic:"",q:"A circuit has 115 V RMS, 2.5A at 60 degrees, what is the power dissipated?.",opts:["79 W.","300 VA.","143 W."],ans:2,exp:"Apparent Power = 115 * 2.5 = 287.5. PF = cos 60 = 0.5. True Power = Apparent Power * PF."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a resonant parallel circuit, current is.",opts:["zero.","minimum.","maximum."],ans:1,exp:"At resonance in a PARALLEL circuit, current is minimum (would be zero if it were not for the resistance in the circuit)."},
    {topic:"",q:"Power factor is.",opts:["sin theta.","tan theta.","cos theta."],ans:2,exp:"Power factor is cos of the phase angle (theta)."},
    {topic:"",q:"Peak factor in a standard AC circuit is.",opts:["1.414 times max. value of applied voltage.","1.11 times max. value of applied voltage.","0.707 time max. value of applied voltage."],ans:0,exp:"Peak Factor (or Crest Factor) = Peak/RMS = 1.414 for a sinusoidal wave."},
    {topic:"",q:"The controlling wave in a CRT is.",opts:["square.","sawtooth.","sinusoidal."],ans:1,exp:"A sawtooth wave is required to control the electron beam vertical and horizontal scan."},
    {topic:"",q:"A wave form having equal amplitude and time base is a.",opts:["rectangular.","sawtooth.","square waveform."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The Sine wave signals of a circular time base are.",opts:["in phase.","phase separated by 90°.","phase separated by 180°."],ans:1,exp:"The two sine wave time bases (X and Y) for a CRT are displaced by 90° to produce a circle on the screen."},
    {topic:"",q:"A differentiator has a time constant that will give.",opts:["a long pulse.","a short pulse.","a continuous output."],ans:1,exp:"A differentiator turns a ramp signal into a steady state (of level corresponding to the slope of the ramp)."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the power factor in a purely resistive circuit?.",opts:["Equal to 1.","Less than 1 but greater than zero.","Greater than 1.","current = voltage. 14. Resistive (R), Capacitive (C) and Inductive (L) Circuits."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In an A.C circuit, what happens if frequency is reduced?.",opts:["Inductive elements may be damaged.","Capacitive elements may be damaged.","There will be no effect."],ans:0,exp:"Lower frequency will decrease the reactance of the inductor which will increase its current, possibly damaging it."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the relationship between the voltage and the current in an A.C circuit containing resistance and inductance?.",opts:["Current lags voltage by 90°.","Current lags voltage by up to 90°.","Current leads voltage by up to 90°."],ans:1,exp:"Remember 'CIVIL'. But lag is not quite 90 degrees because of the resistance in the circuit."},
    {topic:"",q:"In this circuit.",opts:["current leads voltage."],ans:0,exp:"Remember 'CIVIL'."},
    {topic:"",q:"Power factor relates to.",opts:["horsepower and Watts.","KW and KVAR.","true power and apparent power."],ans:2,exp:"Power factor = True Power / Apparent Power."},
    {topic:"",q:"If current lags voltage by 90°, the circuit is.",opts:["resistive.","capacitive.","inductive."],ans:2,exp:"Remember 'CIVIL'."},
    {topic:"",q:"What does the following circuit represent?.",opts:["Low pass filter.","Differentiator.","Integrator."],ans:1,exp:"An RC circuit with the output taken across the resistor is a differentiator."},
    {topic:"",q:"A low frequency supply.",opts:["will make the circuit operate faster due to the reduced impedance.","may damage inductive components.","will have no effect on inductive components."],ans:1,exp:"Lowering the frequency of the current through an inductive component will reduce its reactance and increase the current, possibly burning out the component."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a parallel R, L, C circuit, the value of the capacitor is quadrupled, then the value of the impedance would.",opts:["remain the same.","reduce.","increased."],ans:1,exp:"Increasing the capacitor will decrease the capacitive reactance (and therefore the impedance) - just look at the formula for capacitive reactance. This will decrease the total reactance in the parallel circuit."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a capacitive circuit, if the frequency is increased.",opts:["reactance remains the same.","impedance increases.","the current increases."],ans:2,exp:"Get the formula for capacitive reactance. The current increases because the reactance decreases."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a tuned circuit at resonance, the circuit will be.",opts:["resistive.","capacitive.","inductive."],ans:0,exp:"In a tuned circuit, the inductive reactance and the capacitive reactances cancel each other, leaving just pure resistance."},
    {topic:"",q:"A circuit has a resistance of 50 ohms and an inductance of 0.2 Henry. If it is connected to a 200 volt 50 Hz supply the reactance will be.",opts:["31.42 ohms.","62.84 ohms.","6.284 ohms."],ans:1,exp:"Inductive reactance = 2πfL. 2 * 3.14 * 50 * 0.2 = 62.84 ohms. Since it asks for reactance (not impedance) you can neglect the resistance of 50 ohms."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a vector diagram showing the magnitude and direction of the inductance, capacitance and resistance in an AC circuit.",opts:["the inductive and capacitance reactance would be additive with the resistance subtractive.","it is impossible to show values of this kind using vectors.","the inductive reactance would be in opposition to the capacitive reactance and resistance at 90 degrees."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"At resonant frequency the phase difference between the voltage and current of an AC supply feeding an inductive and capacitive network is.",opts:["90 degrees.","0 degrees.","180 degrees."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An increase in which of the following factors will cause an increase in the inductive reactance of a circuit?.",opts:["Inductance and frequency.","Resistance and capacitive reactance.","Resistance and voltage."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When a circuit with a series inductance and capacitance is at resonant frequency.",opts:["the current in the circuit decreases.","the impedance of the circuit is such that the voltage is in phase with the current.","the impedance of the circuit is such that the voltage leads the current by exactly 90 degrees."],ans:1,exp:"At resonance, the capacitive reactance and the inductive reactance cancel each other, leaving a circuit with a phase angle of zero degrees."},
    {topic:"",q:"A pure capacitor, inductor and resistor are connected in series and the voltage drops across each are 10V and the total current flowing in the circuit is 1A. How much true power is the circuit consuming?.",opts:["30VA.","30W.","10W."],ans:2,exp:"Get the formula for resonant frequency. Tricky calculation to do without a calculator though."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a capacitor 1μF and an inductor 10mH are connected in parallel what is their approximate resonant frequency?.",opts:["1.6 kHz.","62 Hz.","3.2 MHz."],ans:0,exp:"Get the formula for resonant frequency. Tricky calculation to do without a calculator though."},
    {topic:"",q:"Current in an inductive circuit will.",opts:["lag voltage.","lead voltage.","be at unity."],ans:0,exp:"Remember CIVIL."},
    {topic:"",q:"The impedance of a series tuned circuit at resonance is.",opts:["zero.","minimum.","maximum."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In an inductive resistive circuit, 1 amp flows from a supply of 100V dissipating 50 Watts of power. The phase angle is.",opts:["60°.","90°.","45°."],ans:0,exp:"Apparent power = amps * volts = 1 * 100 = 100VA. But True power = 50W. Power Factor = TP/AP = 50/100 = 0.5. Also, PF = COSINE of phase angle. 0.5 = COSINE of phase angle. Phase angle is therefore 60 degrees."},
    {topic:"",q:"The true power developed in this circuit is.",opts:["20W.","50W.","12W."],ans:2,exp:"True power is only in the resistor = I 2 R = 2x2x3 = 12W."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a series LCR circuit at resonance, R = 100 ohms, L and C are each 10 ohms. The total impedance is.",opts:["zero ohms.","100 ohms.","30 ohms."],ans:1,exp:"Resultant reactance (XL-XC) is 10- 10 = 0 ohms. So the total impedance of the circuit is the remaining 100 ohm resistor."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a high Q parallel resonant circuit, what happens to impedance if capacitance is increased by a factor of 4?.",opts:["It increases by factor of 4.","It reduces by 75%.","It remains the same."],ans:1,exp:"Impedance cannot be any higher in a parallel circuit at resonance. If capacitance is changed, circuit will no longer be at resonance and impedance will drop."},
    {topic:"",q:"A parallel circuit at resonance has its impedance at a.",opts:["maximum.","zero.","minimum."],ans:0,exp:"Parallel circuit - impedance is maximum at resonance."},
    {topic:"",q:"The formula for impedance in a series circuit is the root of.",opts:["R 2 + (XC-XL) 2.","R 2 -(XL-XC) 2.","R 2 + (XL-XC) 2."],ans:1,exp:"XL is normally considered to be positive reactance and XC negative reactance. So formula is with XL-XC (not XC-XL)."},
    {topic:"",q:"In this circuit applied voltage is.",opts:["V = IXL.","V = I/R.","V = IXC."],ans:2,exp:"V = I * XC (from Ohm's Law)."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a parallel LCR circuit, if the capacitance is increased by 4 times, what happens to the impedance of the capacitor?.",opts:["increases by 4 times.","remains the same.","decreases by 75%."],ans:2,exp:"Presumably this is referring to the impedance of the capacitor alone, which would reduce to 1/4 as X = 1/2πfC. The impedance of the parallel circuit would reduce also, but how much depends upon the relative size of the other components."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the frequency to a capacitor increases, the current through it.",opts:["decreases.","increases.","is zero."],ans:2,exp:"If it really does mean 'through' it, the answer is zero, as no current goes 'through' a capacitor regardless of its frequency. However, if it means the current in the circuit, the capacitive reactance will go down, thus its current will go UP."},
    {topic:"",q:"If in a electrical circuit there is a difference between the capacitive reactance and the inductive reactance, what would the power factor of the circuit be?.",opts:["More than unity.","Less than unity.","Unity."],ans:1,exp:"Power Factor is always less than one (unity)."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a Series LC circuit if the value of the capacitance is increased then the power factor will be.",opts:["greater than 1.","less than 1.","equal to 1."],ans:1,exp:"The power factor is always less than 1 unless the circuit is at resonance."},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the current in the circuit with a resistance of 8 ohms, inductive reactance of 12 ohms, capacitive reactance of 18 ohms with 20 volts applied across it?.",opts:["10 amps.","1 amp.","2 amps."],ans:2,exp:"Calculate the total impedance Z = √(36 + 64) = 10 and then use formula V = Z * I to find current."},
    {topic:"",q:"A circuit containing a resistor, capacitor and inductor where resonant frequency is met, what will the impedance of the circuit be?.",opts:["Maximum.","Resistance across the resistor.","Zero."],ans:1,exp:"The XL and XC cancel at resonance, leaving only the R."},
    {topic:"",q:"In an alternating current Star wired system, the phase voltage is 115 volts and the line voltage will be approximately.",opts:["200 volts.","115 volts.","163 volts."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An alternating current generator is operating at 50 kVA in a circuit with a power factor of 0.8. The real power is.",opts:["40kW.","72kW.","50kW."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The two factors which govern the output frequency of an AC generator are the.",opts:["the number of poles (in pairs) and the rotor RPM.","strength of the stator field flux and number of poles.","speed of rotation of the rotor and the stator field flux strength."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a delta- connected generator.",opts:["line voltage = root 3 times phase voltage, line current = phase current.","line volts = phase volts, line current = phase current divided by root 3.","line volts = phase volts, line current = root 3 times phase current."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When measuring the phase and line voltages of a generator, it was found that line and phase voltages were equal. The generator is?.",opts:["either delta or star wound.","delta wound.","star wound."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The permanent magnet in an A.C generator induces.",opts:["A.C. in the main generator.","A.C. in the exciter generator.","D.C. in the exciter generator."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"AC generators are rated in.",opts:["kVA.","kVARs.","kW."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The frequency of the output of a 4 pole generator is 400 Hz. The generator is turning at.",opts:["6000 RPM.","12000 RPM.","3000 RPM."],ans:1,exp:"Get the formula for frequency."},
    {topic:"",q:"The voltage induced in the stator of an AC generator is produced by.",opts:["an alternating field.","a rotating magnetic field.","a fixed magnetic field."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A 30 KVA rated generator has a power factor of 0.8. What is its maximum consistent power?.",opts:["30 KW.","37.5 KW.","24 KW."],ans:2,exp:"Power factor = true power / apparent power."},
    {topic:"",q:"The three voltages of a three phase generator are connected.",opts:["in series with each other.","independently of each other.","in parallel with each other."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a 2 phase generator, how far apart are the windings electrically?.",opts:["45°.","180°.","90°."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Where are the output windings on an AC generator?.",opts:["Rotor with slip rings.","Rotor with commutator.","Stator."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The line voltage from a generator with a delta type connection will be.",opts:["lower than the phase voltage.","higher than the phase voltage.","the same as the phase voltage."],ans:2,exp:"Line and phase voltages are the same with a delta connections (it is the current that is different)."},
    {topic:"",q:"An advantage of a star connected generator over a delta connected generator is.",opts:["the line and phase voltages are equal.","two potentials are available.","a balance load is guaranteed."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Permanent magnets in an AC generator are.",opts:["to provide initial excitation.","to control the frequency.","to rectify the current."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The exciter winding in a brushless AC generator is supplied with electrical power from.",opts:["permanent magnets.","a rotating generator integrally mounted on the same shaft as the AC windings.","the busbar."],ans:0,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics Eismin Page 220."},
    {topic:"",q:"An AC generator's frequency will.",opts:["always be constant regardless of the generators drive speed.","be proportional to the RPM at which it is driven.","vary with changes in field strength."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A frequency wild AC generator is used for.",opts:["any AC load.","deicing loads.","instruments and navigation."],ans:1,exp:"Frequency wild can only be used on resistive circuits (heating and lighting)."},
    {topic:"",q:"How many cycles of AC voltage are produced in a six-pole alternator of the revolving-field type, with each revolution of the rotor?.",opts:["Six.","Four.","Three."],ans:2,exp:"Each pair of poles produces 1 cycle."},
    {topic:"",q:"The variable frequency output from an AC generator is used for.",opts:["driving AC operated instruments.","supplying non-inductive loads.","the AC supply instead of using inverters."],ans:1,exp:"Variable frequency (frequency wild) can only be used for resistive loads (i.e. non inductive and non capacitive)."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a single phase AC generator, frequency is determined by.",opts:["the number of pairs of poles.","controlling the current flow through the voltage field windings.","the generator speed."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The amount of electrical power for a given generator weight is.",opts:["greater for AC generator.","determined by the size of the aircraft.","greater for DC generator."],ans:0,exp:"This is one of the advantages of AC power."},
    {topic:"",q:"On a typical AC generator the output is taken.",opts:["direct from the rotor via slip rings.","direct from the stator windings.","direct from the exciter."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An AC generator is producing the required voltage but a higher frequency than that required. To remedy this, the following action must be taken?.",opts:["Decrease the speed of the prime mover.","Decrease the speed of the prime mover, and then decrease the strength of the field.","Decrease the speed of the prime mover, and then increase the strength of the field."],ans:2,exp:"Decreasing the speed of the prime mover decreases the frequency, but it also decreases the output voltage. So you must then return the output voltage to the required level by increasing the generator field strength."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a 3 phase generator with balanced load, the potential between the neutral point and earth is.",opts:["equal to line volts.","zero volts.","equal to phase volts."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The power output of an AC parallel generator system is measured in.",opts:["KW / KVARS.","amperes.","KVA."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The shape of the output waveform of an AC generator is known as a.",opts:["sine wave.","frequency wave.","cosine wave."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The sum of the instantaneous E.M.F.'s in a three phase system is.",opts:["three times the phase voltage.","zero.","equal to the line voltage."],ans:1,exp:"Look at a diagram of 3 phases. Add up all three at any vertical line and it always comes to zero."},
    {topic:"",q:"One revolution of a three phase generator will produce.",opts:["1 cycle.","3 cycles.","2 cycles."],ans:0,exp:"Although there are three phases, they are considered separate - so one cycle is generated per revolution."},
    {topic:"",q:"The 'A' phase of a three phase power system is colour coded.",opts:["red.","blue.","yellow."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The tolerance of the AC generator frequency is.",opts:["10 Hz.","40 Hz.","20 Hz."],ans:2,exp:"+/-10Hz (for paralleling of generators)."},
    {topic:"",q:"The voltage of an AC generator.",opts:["rises to max in one direction and remains.","rises to max in one direction, falls to zero and rises in the same direction.","rises to max in one direction then falls to zero then rises to max in the opposite direction."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A generator supplies 25 A, 4 V and 50 W. What is the apparent power and the power factor?.",opts:["400 VA and 0.5.","100 VA and 2.","100 VA and 0.5."],ans:2,exp:"PF = true power / apparent power. Apparent power = IV = 100 VA. PF = 50/100 = 0.5."},
    {topic:"",q:"A conventional aircraft generator will be.",opts:["delta wound.","series wound.","star wound."],ans:2,exp:"Star wound will give 2 choices of voltage. Aircraft never use series wound generators."},
    {topic:"",q:"Power factor of a generator is.",opts:["tan ø.","cos ø.","sin ø."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a 3 phase generator, what angle are the phases to each other?.",opts:["180°.","120°.","0°."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a 2 phase generator, what angle are the phases to each other?.",opts:["90°.","0°.","180°."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Delta wound line current is.",opts:["equal to phase current.","root 3 * phase current.","less than the phase current."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An alternator delivers 500V RMS at 1 ampere. The power factor is 0.8. The true power is.",opts:["500W.","100VA.","400W."],ans:2,exp:"Apparent Power = amps * volts = 1 * 500 = 500VA. Power Factor = TP/AP. 0.8 = TP/500. TP = 500 * 0.8 = 400W."},
    {topic:"",q:"On a brushless AC generator, the main rotating field is fed with.",opts:["DC via diodes on the rotor.","AC.","AC from the rotating field exciter."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Single phase components in a three phase system may be connected between.",opts:["phase A and B only.","any phase and neutral OR between any two phases.","phase and neutral only."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A generator rated at 30 KVA and power factor 0.8 has a maximum continuous power output of.",opts:["30 kW.","24 kW.","48 kW."],ans:1,exp:"True Power = PF * Apparent Power = 0.8 * 30 = 24kW."},
    {topic:"",q:"If there was a mainly inductive load on an AC generator, the voltage with respect to the current would.",opts:["lag.","be in phase.","lead."],ans:2,exp:"Remember CIVIL."},
    {topic:"",q:"On a brushless AC generator, the permanent magnet generator output is.",opts:["DC.","pulsed.","AC."],ans:2,exp:"The output is AC before it is rectified by the diodes inside the shaft."},
    {topic:"",q:"The output from a rotating armature AC generator would be taken from.",opts:["either the rotor or the stator.","the rotor.","the stator."],ans:1,exp:"View Comment/Reference (if available)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A frequency wild AC generator system has.",opts:["variable voltage only.","variable voltage and frequency.","variable frequency only."],ans:2,exp:"The voltage is held constant by a voltage regulator, which varies the resistance of the field current."},
    {topic:"",q:"An AC generator has four poles. To produce a frequency of 400Hz it must achieve a speed of.",opts:["6000 RPM.","3000 RPM.","12000 RPM."],ans:2,exp:"If it had the minimum of 2 poles (1 pair) it would require 400 revs/sec = 400 * 60 = 24,000 revs/min. With 4 poles (2 pairs), that speed can be halved."},
    {topic:"",q:"The stator of a permanent magnet rotor AC generator.",opts:["is usually star wound.","is wound in series with the field.","is usually delta wound."],ans:0,exp:"Star wound is more common due to the provision of two voltages."},
    {topic:"",q:"The power output of a similar sized 3 phase compared to a single phase generator is.",opts:["more.","same.","less."],ans:0,exp:"3 phase generators are lighter and more compact for their output power."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a voltage equalising circuit the generators are regulated so.",opts:["the higher output is decreased to that of the lower.","the lower is increased and the higher is decreased until they are equal.","the lower output is increased to that of the higher."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a 3-phase delta wound circuit, line current is.",opts:["same as the phase current.","the vectorial sum of all 3 phase currents.","in phase with the phase current."],ans:1,exp:"Line current is root 3 * phase current (rules out a). There is a 30 degree phase shift (rules out b). The root 3 is derived from the vector sum of all three phases."},
    {topic:"",q:"In an aircraft brushless alternator the armature is.",opts:["in series with the field.","delta wound.","star wound."],ans:2,exp:"The armature is normally Star Wound to give a choice of 2 voltages."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the phase voltage of a star wound generator is 115V, what would be the line to line voltage?.",opts:["220V.","180V.","200V."],ans:2,exp:"Line to line voltage means 'Line' voltage. For star wound, Line voltage = 1.73 * phase voltage."},
    {topic:"",q:"What are the factors affecting the frequency of an AC generator?.",opts:["Number of pole pairs and rpm.","Number of turns in the armature coil and number of pole pairs.","Number of turns in the armature coil and rpm."],ans:0,exp:"See formula for frequency of generator output."},
    {topic:"",q:"A generator with more than two phases is known as.",opts:["polyphase.","multiphase.","delta phase."],ans:0,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics. Eismin 5th Edition Page 92."},
    {topic:"",q:"A generator supplies 150VAC phase and load is 10 Ohms per phase. What is the phase current?.",opts:["25.5 A.","1.5 A.","15 A."],ans:2,exp:"I = V/R = 150/10 = 15A."},
    {topic:"",q:"The phases on a three phase AC generator are spaced at.",opts:["120 degrees.","90 degrees.","180 degrees."],ans:0,exp:"The phases are equally spaced."},
    {topic:"",q:"Increasing the load on a permanent magnet generator will.",opts:["reduce the terminal voltage by a large amount.","reduce the terminal voltage by a small amount.","increase the terminal voltage by a small amount."],ans:1,exp:"Aircraft Electricity and Electronics Eismin Pg 193 and Electrical Systems for A&Ps Pg 106."},
    {topic:"",q:"Two 3 phase generators operating in parallel with different phase and line voltages will be connected in.",opts:["Parallel configuration.","star configuration.","delta configuration."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The Stability windings in a 3 phase AC generator are to sense.",opts:["field oscillations.","overvolt.","phase shift."],ans:1,exp:"Stability Winding CAIPs EEL/1-2 3.4.5 (d)."},
    {topic:"",q:"Delta wound alternator currents are.",opts:["the same as line current.","the same as phase current.","the phase current multiplied be the square root of 3."],ans:2,exp:"Delta wound Line current = root 3 * phase current."},
    {topic:"",q:"In smaller single or twin engine aircraft the primary DC power is supplied in the form of rectified output from.",opts:["constant frequency ac generator.","inverter.","frequency wild ac generator."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Single phase components in a 3 phase system may be connected between.",opts:["any phase and earth or between any two phases.","any phase and earth.","phase A and B only."],ans:0,exp:"Eismin - Aircraft Electricity & Electronics - page 220."},
    {topic:"",q:"Under frequency in an AC supply would cause.",opts:["overheating of inductive devices.","overspeeding of AC motors.","overvoltage of capacitive devices."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The line voltage of a 3 phase star connected AC generator is.",opts:["equal to the phase voltage.","greater than the phase voltage.","less than the phase voltage."],ans:1,exp:"Eismin - Aircraft Electricity & Electronics - page 220."},
    {topic:"",q:"In an AC generator of the brushless type the rotating field winding is fed with.",opts:["AC via rotating exciter windings.","DC via the slip rings from an external supply.","DC via diodes from the main exciter winding on the rotor."],ans:2,exp:"Eismin - Aircraft Electricity & Electronics - page 220."},
    {topic:"",q:"An AC generator is rated at 30 kVA at 0.8 Power Factor: this means that the maximum continuous output should not exceed.",opts:["24 KW.","30 KW.","37.5 KW."],ans:0,exp:"Eismin - Aircraft Electricity & Electronics - page 90."},
    {topic:"",q:"To adjust the voltage of an AC generator, whilst maintaining constant frequency, it is necessary to.",opts:["alter the reactance of the starter winding circuit.","alter the field current.","alter the driving speed."],ans:1,exp:"Eismin - Aircraft Electricity & Electronics - page 199."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a Delta connected generator the line current is equal to.",opts:["1.7 * phase voltage.","1.7 * phase current.","phase current."],ans:1,exp:"18. AC Motors."},
    {topic:"",q:"An AC motor which rotates at the same speed as supply frequency is called.",opts:["a universal machine.","a synchronous motor.","an induction motor."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A three phase AC motor is running at speed on constant load. If one phase goes open circuit, the motor will run at.",opts:["same speed.","⅓ speed.","⅔ speed."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Shaded poles in an alternating current motor are intended to.",opts:["reduce eddy current losses.","prevent overshooting.","facilitate starting."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a 3 phase motor, if 1 phase is lost, the motor.",opts:["runs at 2 thirds speed.","runs at 1 third speed.","remains at the same speed."],ans:2,exp:"The speed of a 3-phase motor is dependent only on the frequency of the power supply."},
    {topic:"",q:"A three-phase motor has the windings.",opts:["180°apart.","120° apart.","90° apart."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"To change the direction of a 3-phase induction motor you would.",opts:["swap all of the input connections.","remove one of the input connections.","swap two of the stator connections."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A capacitor in a single phase AC motor is to.",opts:["provide a phase shift.","prevent spikes.","block DC."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The slip speed of an induction motor is.",opts:["stator speed-rotor speed.","stator speed / rotor speed.","stator speed + rotor speed."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An inductance/resistance motor is.",opts:["restricted to low loads only.","less efficient than a capacitance motor.","better than a capacitance motor."],ans:1,exp:"Aircraft Electrical Systems EHJ Pallett Third Edition. Page 143."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a connection to a 3-phase AC motor is disconnected, the motor will.",opts:["slow down and stop.","continue to run.","stop."],ans:1,exp:"3 phase motor, disconnecting a phase will not slow down or stop the motor, since the speed is dependant upon the frequency and the load (slip). It will however have less torque."},
    {topic:"",q:"The phases in supply to a 2 phase motor are electrically.",opts:["120 degrees apart from each other.","180 degrees apart from each other.","90 degrees apart from each other."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Calculate the speed of a 400 Hz, 4 pole machine.",opts:["12,000 rpm.","6000 rpm.","8000 rpm."],ans:0,exp:"See formula for speed of an AC motor."},
    {topic:"",q:"A capacitor in a single phase motor is to.",opts:["prevent sparking at the switch.","provide smoothing.","provide a phase shift."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Slip on a synchronous motor on start is.",opts:["0 %.","100 %.","50 %."],ans:1,exp:"The rotating field commences instantly upon start, but the rotor takes time to 'lock-on'."},
    {topic:"",q:"A shaded pole motor has shaded poles to.",opts:["create a rotating field.","reduce interference.","increase efficiency."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a 2 phase induction motor the control winding is fed with.",opts:["variable phase AC supply.","constant phase AC supply.","DC supply."],ans:0,exp:"A two phase induction motor used on a servo system uses one fixed reference phase and one control phase. The control phase is the same frequency as the reference phase (and is constant) but varies in amplitude. When the amplitude goes negative it is effectively 180 degrees out of phase with the reference phase and the motor turns backwards. More on this subject in module 4."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 3 phase induction motor can be reversed by.",opts:["isolating one phase.","changing over any two phases.","reversing the rotor connections."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The speed of an AC motor can be affected by the.",opts:["field current.","armature current.","pairs of poles."],ans:2,exp:"The number of pairs of poles depends upon the frequency of the supply, which is the only thing which can vary the speed."},
    {topic:"",q:"An AC motor which rotates at the same speed as the supply frequency is called.",opts:["a synchronous motor.","a universal motor.","an induction motor."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a synchronous motor, when the motor is running the slip is.",opts:["0%.","5%.","100%."],ans:0,exp:"Synchronous motors have no slip, at any time."},
    {topic:"",q:"In a two-phase induction motor there are.",opts:["3 poles.","4 poles.","2 poles."],ans:1,exp:"A pair of poles per phase."},
    {topic:"",q:"The speed of a single phase induction motor is dependant on.",opts:["the strength of the armature current.","the number of pairs of poles.","the strength of the field."],ans:1,exp:"Any AC motor's speed is determined by the supply frequency and/or the number of poles. A stronger field or armature current results in only more torque but not speed. 49.html"},
    {topic:"",q:"Motor slip is.",opts:["the percentage of difference in speeds between the stator and rotor fields.","a symptom of a weak field.","a shifting of the MNA of the motor."],ans:0,exp:"Motor slip is the percentage of difference in speeds between the stator and rotor fields."},
    {topic:"",q:"The principal characteristic of a synchronous motor is.",opts:["it must have a synchronised 3 phase supply to operate.","it rotates at a speed that is synchronised with the applied dc current.","it rotates at a speed that is synchronised with the applied ac current."],ans:2,exp:"A synchronous motor rotates at a speed that is synchronised with the applied ac current."},
    {topic:"",q:"A simple basic induction motor has.",opts:["good starting torque, poor running torque.","poor starting torque, good running torque.","poor starting torque, poor running torque."],ans:1,exp:"Starting torque is usually about 60% of maximum. See forum topic 'Induction Motors' for more info."},
    {topic:"",q:"Which of the following applications would require a 'continuously rated' motor?.",opts:["Fuel pump motor.","Flap drive actuator.","Cargo door actuator."],ans:0,exp:"Most pump motors are 'continuous rated' to provide a constant output."},
    {topic:"",q:"A centrifugal speed switch is used.",opts:["to cut out the resistor/inductor circuit on an AC motor.","to cut out the capacitor circuit on an AC motor.","to cut out the slow start resistor on an AC motor."],ans:0,exp:"Pallett Aircraft Electrical Systems 3rd Edition Page 143."},
    {topic:"",q:"Disconnecting one of the phases from a 3-phase motor will cause the motor to.",opts:["run at the same speed.","run at 2/3 speed.","stop."],ans:0,exp:"It will run at the same speed."},
    {topic:"",q:"The controlling phase on a two phase motor is of a.",opts:["variable amplitude.","fixed amplitude.","direct current."],ans:0,exp:"Pg 141 Aircraft instruments and Integrated Systems Pallett."},
    {topic:"",q:"The difference in speed between a synchronous motor's rotor and stator is known as.",opts:["phase lag.","rotor lag.","slip speed."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the purpose of the capacitor used in a single phase AC motor?.",opts:["Smoothing.","Changing direction of motor.","Provides a high starting torque."],ans:2,exp:"19."},
    {topic:"",q:"Three 5- microfarad capacitors are connected in parallel. What is there total capacitance?.",opts:["15 microfarad.","5 microfarad.","1.66 microfarad."],ans:0,exp:"Capacitance in parallel add up directly."},
    {topic:"",q:"When capacitors are joined in parallel, the total capacitance is.",opts:["less than the smallest of the individual capacitors.","equal to the sum of the individual capacitors.","dependant upon the voltage applied."],ans:1,exp:"Capacitors in series/parallel theory, is the opposite to resistors in series/parallel theory."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the distance between the two plates of a capacitor is reduced, what will be the effect?.",opts:["Capacitance increases.","Capacitance decreases.","Capacitance is unaffected."],ans:0,exp:"Get a formula for the capacitance of a capacitor. See that 'd' is inversely proportional to the capacitance."},
    {topic:"",q:"The strength of the EMF generated by a DC generator is given the formula: E = EMF, B = flux density, l = length of conductors in the armature, V = velocity of conductors.",opts:["E = (B*l)/V.","E = B*l*V.","E = (l*V)/B."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The rotating part of a motor/generator is called.",opts:["commutator.","armature.","yoke."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"No EMF is induced in the loop of a generator when.",opts:["it is moving at right angles to the field.","it is moving parallel to the field.","the field is at a minimum."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The instantaneous value of the EMF induced in a rotating wire loop is.",opts:["directly proportional to the sine of the angle between the loop and the field.","indirectly proportional to the sine of the angle between the loop and the field.","directly proportional to the cosine of the angle between the loop and the field."],ans:2,exp:"When the loop is laying parallel with the field (0o) there is maximum EMF. Cos0o = 1."},
    {topic:"",q:"One purpose of the commutator is to.",opts:["switch the direction of the current flow every half cycle.","switch the direction of current flow every cycle.","switch the direction of current flow every two cycles."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The power output of a DC generator is governed primarily by.",opts:["its speed of rotation.","its ability to dissipate heat.","its method of construction."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A DC machine has internal resistance due to.",opts:["armature windings, brushes and brushes to commutator contact.","armature windings, brushes and commutator inertia.","armature windings and brushes."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Compensation windings are fitted to a generator in.",opts:["series with the field windings.","parallel with armature windings.","series with armature windings."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Armature reaction has the effect of.",opts:["reducing the output by displacing the geometrical neutral axis.","reducing the output by distorting the magnetic field.","reducing the output by increasing internal resistance."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Reactive sparking maybe overcome by.",opts:["decreasing the time constant of the armature by increasing its resistance.","increasing the time constant of the armature by decreasing its resistance.","increasing the time constant of the armature by increasing its resistance."],ans:0,exp:"The ratio of armature inductance to its resistance is the electrical time constant of a brush type motor."}
